window.MASTERY_SEARCH = [{"title": "01.01 · Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost", "url": "v01-c01.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "The problem economics begins with You have one evening. You can work for pay, study, spend time with family, or rest. Each option uses the same limited hours. The problem is not necessarily a shortage of money; it is that you cannot simultaneously do everything you value. Economics studies choices made under constraints, the interactions among those choices, and their consequences. A constraint describes what is feasible. A household faces time, income, borrowing and information constraints. A firm faces technology, capacity, financing and contractual constraints. A government faces resources, administrative capabilities, political commitments and legal constraints. Start an analysis by identifying the decision maker, the available actions, the constraints, and the objective. An objective is what the decision maker values. It might be consumption, profit, health, learning, reliability, or a combination. A financial return is one possible component. It is not a universal measure of wellbeing. When important outcomes cannot be expressed credibly in money, keep them explicit rather than assigning invented dollar values. Opportunity cost is an alternative The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the best feasible alternative forgone. It is not necessarily an invoice or cash payment. Using a building you already own can have an opportunity cost because you could rent it out. Studying for three hours can have an opportunity cost because you could work or rest. The best alternative must be realistic and available. Suppose you can choose only one of three activities with net benefits of 100, 70 and 40 measured in the same units. If you choose the first, its opportunity cost is 70. Adding 70 and 40 would pretend you could undertake both forgone alternatives even though the constraint permits only one. The advantage of the first choice over the best alternative is 30. Be clear about the accounting convention. If you have already subtracted the forgone alternative's value when calculating an economic net benefit, subtracting it again would double count it. A useful approach is to calculate the future benefits and costs of each feasible action consistently, then compare their net values. Worked example 1 — Work, study, or rest You have four hours. Paid work earns $25 an hour but requires a $10 transportation expense. You estimate the value of studying during the same four hours at $130. Rest has an estimated value of $80. Assume the three values are comparable monetary equivalents, and the actions are mutually exclusive. Work's net value is 4 × $25 − $10 = $90. Study's net value is $130; rest's is $80. The best alternative to study is work, worth $90. Study's opportunity cost is $90. Study has a $130 − $90 = $40 advantage over the best alternative. The recommendation is conditional on those valuations. If the work is unavailable, it cannot be the opportunity cost. If studying causes exhaustion that was omitted from its valuation, the comparison needs correction. If rest and paid work can be combined in a different feasible schedule, that schedule must also be evaluated. Relevant costs and sunk costs A cost is sunk at the time of a decision when it has already been incurred and cannot be recovered through any currently available action. A past payment does not automatically qualify: if you can obtain a refund or sell what you purchased, the recoverable value affects the decision. Compare consequences that differ among the actions from the decision time forward. Suppose a business spent $40,000 developing a product. Launching now would generate $30,000 of revenue and require another $20,000 of cost. Abandoning would generate no future revenue or expense. With no other consequences, launching has a $10,000 advantage. The unrecoverable $40,000 affects the historical project's total return, but it does not reverse the current comparison. Historical accountability and current decision quality answer different questions. Managers should learn why the development money was spent and whether the original decision was justified. They should also choose the best remaining action. Continuing a bad project merely to make the past expenditure feel worthwhile can destroy more value. Past choices can still matter through their present consequences. A signed contract can impose a future cancellation charge. An acquired skill can improve a future benefit. A reputation can influence demand. Count these consequences if they differ between the actions; do not label all history irrelevant. Think at the margin An average describes a total divided by a quantity. A marginal quantity describes the change caused by an additional unit. A firm deciding whether to make one more item needs the additional revenue and additional cost, rather than the average historical profit of all items. For discrete actions, write the comparison explicitly: Incremental net benefit = incremental benefit − incremental cost An additional unit is worth taking when this quantity is positive, given the constraints and the consequences already included. When benefits decrease and costs increase with quantity, this often means expanding until the next unit is no longer worthwhile. Without those conditions, a local comparison need not find the best overall decision; compare the feasible alternatives directly. Later, calculus will formalize marginal changes with derivatives. The familiar condition “marginal benefit equals marginal cost” applies to particular interior, differentiable optimization problems. It is not a rule that every optimum must satisfy. Capacity constraints, indivisible choices and corner solutions often produce an optimum where equality fails. Worked example 2 — A partially completed workshop You already paid a nonrefundable $60 registration fee for a workshop. Attending requires another $10 transportation payment. You value the remaining workshop at $45. Staying home and working earns $40 net. Assume no additional benefits or costs. Current action Future benefit Future cash cost Net value Attend $45 $10 $35 Work instead $40 $0 $40 Working is better by $5. The $60 already paid is common to both actions and cancels from their comparison. Equivalently, attending provides $45 and costs $10 plus a $40 forgone alternative, for an incremental advantage of −$5. These are two consistent calculations of the same choice. Now suppose a refund of $20 is available only if you do not attend. The alternative becomes worth $60, and attending is worse by $25. The registration payment has a recoverable component and is no longer entirely sunk. A decision procedure you can reuse First specify the choice and the decision time. List realistic alternatives, including doing nothing when it is feasible. Then describe the consequences that differ among them. Put money on a common date when timing matters; Chapter 5 develops that calculation. Identify uncertain outcomes rather than silently treating them as certain; Chapter 8 develops scenarios. Check for double counting. A lost wage can appear as the alternative's benefit or as an opportunity cost of the chosen action, but it should not appear twice in the same comparison. An expense already deducted from profit should not be subtracted again. Nonmonetary outcomes should remain visible when monetary equivalents are unreliable. Finally state a conditional conclusion: which action is best under the assumptions, which assumptions matter most, and what new information could change the result. A disciplined decision is transparent about its limits. It need not pretend that uncertain personal values or future outcomes have been measured precisely. Common errors Counting only cash: overlooks the best use of time, owned assets, or scarce capacity. Adding incompatible alternatives: inflates opportunity cost beyond what was feasible. Counting the same loss twice: mixes a net-value comparison with an opportunity-cost adjustment inconsistently. Letting a sunk payment decide: confuses historical performance with the best current action. Using averages for additional decisions: ignores the consequences of the next unit. Inventing certainty: converts uncertain or nonmonetary values into precise-looking numbers without justification. Exercises Attempt each problem before opening the solutions. State your assumptions and units. You can work for five hours at $18 per hour with a $15 transportation cost, study for an estimated value of $95, or rest for an estimated value of $65. The choices are exclusive. Which is best, what is its opportunity cost, and what is its advantage? An owned office can be rented out for $900 per month after rental expenses. Using it for your own business requires $150 per month in additional cleaning costs. What monthly cost should the business compare with the benefits of occupying it? Explain why the historical purchase price is not enough information. A nonrefundable $120 ticket has already been purchased. Attending provides a benefit of $90 and costs $20 in transportation. An alternative activity provides $80 net. Compare the current choices. How does the result change if the ticket can be resold for $30 after fees? A project already cost $8,000. Completing it requires $2,000 and produces $2,700 of future revenue. Abandonment has no future consequence. Calculate the best current decision and the total project profit if completed. Explain why these can point to different judgments. The net benefits of three mutually exclusive actions are 160, 120 and 90. Someone says the first action's opportunity cost is 210. Identify the error and calculate the relevant comparison. A seller can produce up to four units. Additional-unit benefits are 30, 25, 18 and 10; additional-unit costs are 12, 15, 17 and 20. Assuming these are the complete consequences, how many units should be produced? Calculate the total net benefit. A student says, “I must finish this course because I have already spent 100 hours on it.” Give a case where continuing is rational and one where stopping is rational. Identify which future consequences would decide the comparison. Write a decision memo of no more than 150 words about a real choice. Identify the decision time, two feasible alternatives, the opportunity cost, a potentially sunk cost, uncertainty, and a fact that could change your decision. Do not invent monetary values for outcomes you cannot credibly price. Solutions and evaluation criteria Work is worth 5 × 18 − 15 = $75. Study is best at $95; its opportunity cost is $75, and its advantage is $20. Rest is not added to work because the forgone alternatives are exclusive. The relevant monthly cost is $900 forgone net rent plus $150 additional cleaning, or $1,050, assuming those consequences are distinct and complete. Historical purchase price does not describe the current best alternative. If selling is also feasible, compare its future consequences on a consistent horizon. Attendance is worth 90 − 20 = $70; the alternative is $80. Choose the alternative by $10. With resale available only if you do not attend, the alternative is worth $110 and is better by $40. The resale opportunity changes the current comparison. Complete: 2,700 − 2,000 = $700 more future value than abandonment. Total project profit is 2,700 − 8,000 − 2,000 = −$7,300. The original project lost money, yet completion minimizes the remaining loss under these assumptions. The analyst added alternatives that cannot both be taken. The best forgone alternative is 120; the first action's advantage is 160 − 120 = 40. Marginal net benefits are 18, 10, 1 and −10. Produce three units for a total net benefit of 29. The complete quantity comparison gives totals 0, 18, 28, 29 and 19, confirming the choice. Continuing is rational if its expected future benefits exceed those of the best alternative after remaining costs. Stopping is rational if an alternative has higher future net value. The 100 unrecoverable hours are sunk, but skills already acquired, remaining fees, completion requirements and future opportunities can affect the comparison. A strong memo compares genuinely feasible choices, uses a consistent time horizon, separates recoverable from sunk costs, avoids double counting, preserves important nonmonetary outcomes, and gives a conditional recommendation. There is no universal correct personal choice; the quality of the reasoning is assessable. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain opportunity cost to someone without economic training using an owned asset and a time allocation. Explain why a disappointing past investment can still rationally receive more spending. Delayed check: a month later, revisit an actual choice. Reconstruct the alternatives without reading this chapter. Can you distinguish the best forgone alternative, sunk costs, and future consequences that genuinely differ? Where this leads Later chapters formalize constraints and objectives with algebra, optimization, probability and dynamic models. Welfare economics asks whose benefits and costs are counted; information economics asks how knowledge changes choice; institutional analysis asks which choices are actually available. The discipline begins with a correct comparison."}, {"title": "01.02 · Numbers, units and percentage changes", "url": "v01-c02.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Numbers need a meaning A number without a unit, time period or denominator can mislead. Revenue of “50” could mean $50, $50 million, fifty units sold, or an index reading. Growth of “5” could mean five dollars, five percent or five percentage points. Before calculating, identify what each quantity measures and the population or period to which it applies. Write quantities in forms such as “$50 million per year,” “12 unemployed people per 100 labor-force participants,” or “$30 per item.” Units discipline the calculation. Dividing dollars by dollars yields a ratio. Dividing dollars by units yields dollars per unit. Adding a monthly rate to a yearly rate without conversion is a mistake even if a calculator accepts it. Fractions, ratios and decimals A fraction expresses a part relative to a whole: 3/4 means three parts when the whole is divided into four equal parts. A ratio compares quantities. Debt of $60 and equity of $40 produce a debt-to-equity ratio of 60/40 = 1.5, while debt's share of total financing is 60/(60 + 40) = 0.6. These answer different questions. A percentage is a ratio multiplied by 100. The decimal 0.06 equals 6%; 1.06 equals 106%; 0.006 equals 0.6%. When using a formula, translate percentages into decimal rates unless the formula explicitly expects percentage units. Multiplying $1,000 by 6 yields $6,000, whereas multiplying it by a 6% rate gives $60. Financial quantities can be negative. A loss of $20 relative to a $100 initial investment is a −20% return. However, percentage changes from zero are undefined, and changes from a negative base require careful interpretation. A business's profit moving from −$10 to +$10 is a $20 improvement; describing it as an ordinary −200% growth rate would obscure the economic meaning. Levels, changes and growth rates The absolute change is the new level minus the old level. The ordinary percentage change uses the old level as the denominator: Absolute change = new − old Percentage change = 100 × (new − old) / old This definition requires a nonzero base and is most straightforward for positive levels. An increase from 80 to 100 is 20 units and 25%. Reversing the move from 100 to 80 is −20 units and −20%. Percentage changes are asymmetric because their denominators differ. The fraction remaining after a loss matters for recovery. A 50% loss leaves half the original value. Doubling the remaining half requires a 100% gain. More generally, after a fractional loss L with 0 ≤ L < 1, recovering to the original level requires a gain of L/(1 − L). A complete loss cannot be recovered by applying a finite percentage return to the zero remaining balance. Worked example 1 — Growth, losses and changing denominators A business's monthly sales rise from $80,000 to $100,000, then fall back to $80,000. The increase is $20,000/$80,000 = 0.25, or 25%. The decrease is −$20,000/$100,000 = −0.20, or −20%. The combined growth factor is 1.25 × 0.80 = 1.00. Sales return to the initial level. Adding the rates gives 25% − 20% = 5%, which is not the combined percentage change. For consecutive changes, multiply growth factors. If a quantity rises 10% and then 20%, its factor is 1.10 × 1.20 = 1.32, a 32% increase. The extra two percentage points arise because the second change applies to a larger base. Percentages and percentage points When the measured quantity is itself a percentage, distinguish an absolute difference in percentage units from a relative percentage change. If an interest rate rises from 4% to 6%, it rises by two percentage points . Relative to the original 4% rate, it rises by (6 − 4)/4 = 50%. A basis point is one hundredth of a percentage point. One percentage point is 100 basis points. A rate increase from 4.00% to 4.25% is 0.25 percentage points, 25 basis points, and a 6.25% relative increase in the rate. The phrases are not interchangeable. Suppose a quoted unemployment rate falls from 10% to 9%. That is a decline of one percentage point or 10% relative to its previous rate. It does not establish that the number of unemployed people fell by 10%, because the labor-force denominator may also have changed. Reconstruct the counts when that is the question. Weighted averages An ordinary average gives each observation equal weight. A weighted average assigns specified weights that sum to one: Weighted average = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ with w₁ + w₂ + … + wₙ = 1 To combine a rate across groups, the relevant weights typically correspond to the rate's denominator. Combining employment shares uses group populations in the applicable population measure. Combining profit margins uses revenues. A large and a small division should not receive equal weights merely because each is one division. For a portfolio measured over one common period with no intermediate flows, the simple portfolio return is the initial-value-weighted average of component simple returns. This statement does not imply that averaging yearly returns gives compound growth. Chapter 5 develops the distinction between growth over time and aggregation across assets. Worked example 2 — Combining profit margins correctly Division A has revenue of $100 and profit of $20. Division B has revenue of $900 and profit of $45. Assume the revenues and profits are measured over the same period and can be added without interdivision double counting. A's margin is 20/100 = 20%. B's is 45/900 = 5%. The equal-weight average is (20% + 5%)/2 = 12.5%. It gives the small division the same importance as the large one. The business's actual combined margin is (20 + 45)/(100 + 900) = 65/1,000 = 6.5%. Revenue weights are 0.1 and 0.9. The weighted margin is 0.1 × 20% + 0.9 × 5% = 6.5%. The method follows the denominator. If the objective were to describe a typical division's margin rather than the combined business's margin, the equal-weight average might answer that different question. Good analysis states which question it is answering. Rates across time and measurement conventions A rate needs its period. A monthly 1% return and an annual 1% return do not describe the same growth. Multiplying a monthly rate by 12 gives a simple annualized convention, not the exact effective annual growth when monthly gains compound. At 1% each month, that growth is (1.01) 12 − 1, approximately 12.68%. When reading a number, ask whether it is a level, an annual total, a monthly flow, a seasonally adjusted rate, or an index. A flow observed for one period cannot automatically be treated as a forecast for the next. A quarter's sales multiplied by four is an annualized representation, not necessarily the year's realized sales. Unit conversions must preserve the quantity. One million dollars equals 1,000 thousand dollars. If a dataset reports money in thousands and you treat it as dollars, the result will be off by a factor of 1,000. When dividing two monetary quantities, check both scales before forming a ratio. Precision, rounding and plausibility Do not round every intermediate calculation unless the contract or measurement convention requires it. Preserve sufficient precision, then round the displayed answer to a level justified by the inputs and the purpose. A forecast based on rough assumptions does not become more reliable when shown to eight decimal places. Use plausibility checks. A margin combining positive revenues should fall between the individual margins if all relevant profits and revenues are aggregated consistently. A 10% increase followed by a 10% decrease should leave slightly less than the initial amount: 1.10 × 0.90 = 0.99. If a conversion makes an ordinary household budget equal to national income, inspect the scale before advancing to a complicated model. Numerical competence is largely disciplined interpretation. The formula is often simple; the challenge is identifying the correct base, denominator, period, population and unit. Exercises A wage rises from $20 to $23 per hour. Calculate its absolute and percentage change. Then calculate the percentage reduction required to return from $23 to $20. A rate rises from 3.50% to 4.10%. Express the change in percentage points, basis points and relative percentage terms. An asset worth $200 loses 30% and then gains 30%. Find the final value and total percentage change. What gain after the initial loss would restore the original value? Division X has revenue of $400 and profit of $40. Division Y has revenue of $600 and profit of $120. Find the divisions' margins, the equal-weight mean margin and the combined margin. A portfolio begins with $300 in asset A and $700 in asset B. During one common period, A returns 10% and B returns −5%, with no external cash flows. Calculate the portfolio's ending value and simple return. A dataset lists debt as 2,400 in thousands of dollars and GDP as 3 in millions of dollars. Calculate debt in dollars and the debt-to-GDP ratio. Explain the unit correction. At time 0, a labor force of 1,000 includes 100 unemployed people. At time 1, a labor force of 1,200 includes 108 unemployed people. Calculate both unemployment rates, their percentage-point change, and the percentage change in the unemployed count. A report states, “Profit increased from −$5 million to $10 million, so profit grew −300%.” Explain the arithmetic behind that expression, why it misleads, and how you would report the result. Add a rule for reporting changes when the original level is zero. Solutions and evaluation criteria The wage rises $3 per hour, or 3/20 = 15%. The reverse change is −3/23, approximately −13.04%. The denominators differ. The increase is 0.60 percentage points or 60 basis points. Relative to 3.50%, it is 0.60/3.50, approximately 17.14%. The ending value is 200 × 0.70 × 1.30 = $182, a −9% total change. Recovery from the $140 remaining value requires 60/140 = 42.857…%, approximately 42.86%. X's margin is 10%; Y's is 20%. The equal-weight mean is 15%. Combined profit is $160 on $1,000 revenue, giving a 16% margin. Revenue weighting produces 0.4 × 10% + 0.6 × 20% = 16%. A ends at $330 and B at $665. Total is $995, a −0.5% return on $1,000. Initial-value weighting gives 0.3 × 10% + 0.7 × (−5%) = −0.5%. Debt is 2,400 × $1,000 = $2.4 million. GDP is $3 million. The ratio is 2.4/3 = 0.8 or 80%. Dividing the unconverted displayed values would mix scales. The initial rate is 100/1,000 = 10%; the later rate is 108/1,200 = 9%. The rate falls one percentage point even though the unemployed count rises by 8%. A changing denominator explains the difference. The ordinary formula gives (10 − (−5))/(−5) = −3. Reporting −300% conceals the improvement because the base is negative. State that the business moved from a $5 million loss to a $10 million profit, a $15 million improvement. With a zero initial level, ordinary percentage growth is undefined; report the absolute change and relevant context. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why “the rate rose 50%” and “the rate rose two percentage points” can both describe the same event. Give your own numbers and identify the denominator. Delayed check: reconstruct the return after a 20% loss followed by a 20% gain, and explain the result without a memorized formula. Where this leads Algebra will express these relationships symbolically. Accounting will make denominator choices more consequential. Statistics will formalize sampling weights and uncertainty. Asset pricing will distinguish simple returns, logarithmic returns, compound growth and cash-flow-sensitive performance measures. Accurate units and bases remain essential at every level."}, {"title": "01.03 · Algebra, functions and graphs", "url": "v01-c03.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Turn a verbal claim into a relationship A model becomes easier to examine when its quantities and relationships are explicit. Suppose a taxi fare consists of a $4 starting charge plus $2 per kilometer. Let x be kilometers traveled and y be dollars charged. The rule is y = 4 + 2x. It makes a testable prediction, specifies units, and exposes an assumption: the per-kilometer charge is constant over the trip. A variable can take different values. A parameter is treated as fixed for a particular exercise. Here x varies; the numbers 4 and 2 are parameters. A parameter can change between scenarios: a new tariff could change the starting charge. Always distinguish a quantity chosen by an actor, a quantity determined within the model, and an input taken as given. An equation says two expressions are equal. An identity holds by the definitions involved, such as total spending equaling the sum of spending categories. A behavioral equation describes how an actor responds, such as a proposed relationship between price and quantity demanded. The mathematical equality sign does not make a behavioral relationship an accounting truth. Solve equations while preserving equality Perform the same legitimate operation on both sides. To solve 4 + 2x = 18, subtract 4 from both sides to obtain 2x = 14, then divide both sides by 2 to obtain x = 7. Substitute 7 into the original expression: 4 + 2 × 7 = 18. Substitution checks the answer and can detect algebraic mistakes. Division requires a nonzero divisor. Multiplying both sides by zero destroys information. Squaring both sides can introduce solutions that did not satisfy the original equation, so check candidates afterward. If an equation represents an economic problem, mathematical solutions must also satisfy its domain and constraints. A negative number of purchased books may solve a rearranged expression but still be infeasible. An inequality describes a feasible range. A budget of $20 with books priced at $5 and meals at $2 permits nonnegative quantities x and y satisfying: 5x + 2y ≤ 20 x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0 Equality identifies bundles that spend the entire budget. Inequality also permits unspent money. Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number reverses its direction: −2x ≤ 6 implies x ≥ −3. The reversal follows from the ordering of negative numbers, not from an arbitrary convention. Functions, domains and slopes A function assigns one output to each input in its domain. The notation y = f(x) means that y is the output associated with x. For f(x) = 4 + 2x, f(3) = 10. Domain restrictions describe allowed inputs: distance is nonnegative, and a fare rule might apply only within a service area. A linear function has the form y = a + bx. The intercept a is the output when x = 0. The slope b is the change in output per additional unit of input. More generally, the slope between two points is: Slope = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁), for x₂ ≠ x₁ The slope carries units. A fare slope of 2 means $2 per kilometer. A budget slope describing meals against books means meals forgone per additional book. Changing units changes the numerical slope without changing the underlying relationship. A distance measured in meters rather than kilometers gives a fare slope of $0.002 per meter. A positive slope means the modeled quantities move in the same direction; a negative slope means they move in opposite directions. Neither sign establishes causation in observed data. A model specifying a causal mechanism and a graph of an observed association are different objects. Worked example 1 — Read a budget line With a $20 budget, books at $5 each and meals at $2 each, the full-spending boundary is 5x + 2y = 20. Rearrange: 2y = 20 − 5x y = 10 − 2.5x If x = 0, then y = 10: all money goes to meals. If y = 0, then x = 4: all money goes to books. If x = 2, then y = 5. Spending is 5 × 2 + 2 × 5 = $20. The slope is −2.5 meals per book. Another book costs as much as 2.5 meals. Budget line for books and meals The feasible boundary slopes from zero books and ten meals to four books and zero meals. Two books and five meals lies on the line. Bundles below the line are feasible under the budget. 0 2 4 10 5 Books (x) Meals (y) (2, 5) Feasible bundles The line is a constraint, not a prediction of what the consumer chooses. The pictured line permits fractional goods as a mathematical representation. If only whole books and meals are available, restrict x and y to nonnegative integers. The line still describes the cost trade-off, but some points are unavailable. A preference model is needed to predict which feasible point the consumer prefers. Graphs and inverse relationships A graph labels an input-output relationship on axes. Always inspect the labels, units and ranges before interpreting it. The same relationship can look very different when an axis is truncated or its scale is changed. A vertical line can describe an equation in two variables, but it is not an ordinary function y = f(x) because one input would correspond to many outputs. An invertible relationship can be rewritten with the other variable as the output. If quantity demanded is Q = 120 − 2P, solving for price gives P = 60 − Q/2. The slope of quantity with respect to price is −2 units per dollar; the slope of price with respect to quantity is −0.5 dollars per unit. These are different units and correspond to different axis arrangements. Economics often places price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis even when a demand equation is written as Q(P). State the inverse form when interpreting that graph. Do not assume that the displayed graph's slope is the coefficient in the original equation. Worked example 2 — Solve a simple market model Suppose buyers demand Q d = 120 − 2P and sellers supply Q s = 20 + 3P, measured in units per day and dollars per unit. The proposed equilibrium has equal quantities demanded and supplied. Set 120 − 2P = 20 + 3P. Subtract 20 and add 2P on both sides: 100 = 5P. Divide by 5: P = $20 per unit. Substitute in each equation: Q d = 120 − 40 = 80 and Q s = 20 + 60 = 80. At P = $10, demand is 100 while supply is 50. The model has excess demand of 50 units at that price. Explaining how price actually adjusts requires assumptions about bargaining, contracts, inventories and institutions. Solving an equality does not itself establish the adjustment process or prove that the equations fit a real market. Nonlinear relationships Not every relationship has a constant slope. If total cost is C(q) = 10 + q 2 , increasing output from 1 to 2 raises cost from 11 to 14, an additional 3. Increasing output from 2 to 3 raises cost from 14 to 19, an additional 5. The same output increment has a different cost consequence at a different starting point. Compounding uses powers. An amount growing at rate r for n periods becomes its initial amount multiplied by (1 + r) n . A logarithm reverses exponentiation: if a x = b with appropriate positive values and a ≠ 1, then x = log a (b). Logarithms will help solve growth and timing problems. They do not permit the real logarithm of a negative quantity. Piecewise functions describe rules that change across ranges. A cost can be $5 per unit up to capacity and $8 per extra unit afterward. Write the ranges explicitly. Ignoring a threshold can produce an incorrect optimum even when the arithmetic within each range is correct. Check the economic interpretation After solving, verify equality or inequality in the original expressions. Then check the units, domain, constraints and meaning. Ask which parameters were fixed and whether their values are plausible. A mathematical result is conditional on the specification. The equation is a language for reasoning, not a substitute for it. A precise model can expose a mistaken claim, but it can also produce a precise answer to the wrong question. Tie each symbol back to a measured or conceptual economic quantity. Exercises A service charges $6 plus $3 per hour. Write its charge as a function of hours, calculate the charge for four hours, and solve for the hours corresponding to a $27 charge. State the units of its slope. A $60 budget purchases goods x at $10 each and y at $5 each. Write the feasible set and full-spending boundary. Find both intercepts and the slope of y against x. Is (4, 5) feasible? Solve 7 + 4x = 31 and verify your answer by substitution. Then solve −3x ≤ 12 and explain why the inequality direction changes. Demand is Q d = 150 − 3P and supply is Q s = 30 + 2P. Find equilibrium price and quantity. Calculate excess demand at P = 20. Rewrite Q = 80 − 4P with P as a function of Q. Give the slope and units for each representation. State a nonnegative-quantity domain for P. For C(q) = 20 + 2q + q 2 , calculate costs at q = 0, 1, 2 and 3 and the additional cost of each unit. Does a constant additional cost describe this rule? A $100 amount grows by 10% per period. Write the amount after n periods and calculate it after two periods. Solve 100 × 1.1 n = 121 by recognizing the power. Explain why n is a period count. A graph shows sales and advertising moving together. Explain what its slope could describe and why that relationship alone does not show that more advertising causes the observed sales increase. Propose one alternative explanation. Solutions and evaluation criteria Charge f(h) = 6 + 3h dollars. Four hours costs $18. Set 6 + 3h = 27 to obtain h = 7 hours. The slope is $3 per hour. The set is 10x + 5y ≤ 60 with x,y ≥ 0. The boundary is y = 12 − 2x, with intercepts (0,12) and (6,0). The slope is −2 y-units per x-unit. Bundle (4,5) costs $65 and is infeasible. 4x = 24, so x = 6; 7 + 4 × 6 = 31. Dividing the inequality by −3 gives x ≥ −4. Values at or above −4 satisfy the original inequality. 150 − 3P = 30 + 2P implies 120 = 5P, hence P = 24 and Q = 78. At P = 20, demand is 90 and supply is 70, giving excess demand of 20. P = 20 − Q/4. The Q-on-P slope is −4 quantity units per dollar; the P-on-Q slope is −0.25 dollars per quantity unit. For nonnegative prices and quantities, 0 ≤ P ≤ 20. Costs are 20, 23, 28 and 35. Additional costs are 3, 5 and 7. The function is nonlinear and its additional cost increases over these quantities. The amount is 100 × 1.1 n . After two periods it is $121, so n = 2 solves the equation. The base is the per-period growth factor; the exponent counts applications of that factor. The slope describes an association given the graph's measurement and scales. Firms may advertise more when they expect unusually high demand, or seasonal factors may increase both advertising and sales. A causal claim needs a credible counterfactual and a design that handles confounding. Several explanations are acceptable if their mechanisms are clear. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the budget diagram without using the word “slope” until you have described what one more book requires giving up. Then connect that description to the equation. Delayed check: invent a new two-good budget and market-clearing problem, solve each from scratch, and verify your results in the original equations. Where this leads Calculus studies nonlinear marginal changes and optimization. Linear algebra handles many equations simultaneously. Econometrics estimates relationships from data and examines their interpretation. Throughout these developments, the essential habit is to state the variables, units, domain, assumptions and question before solving."}, {"title": "01.04 · Stocks, flows and balance sheets", "url": "v01-c04.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A snapshot is different from a rate of activity A stock is measured at a point in time. Examples include cash held at the end of a day, outstanding debt on a reporting date, inventory in a warehouse, and the population living in a country. A flow is measured over an interval. Examples include wages earned during a month, purchases made during a quarter, interest charged during a year, and births during a period. Units often expose the distinction. A stock might be $10,000 at 31 December. A flow might be $1,000 per month or $12,000 earned over a year. Comparing a debt stock with annual income is meaningful if the ratio is described as debt relative to one year's income. It is not a statement that debt and income are the same kind of quantity. A frequent mistake is to treat income as wealth. A household can have a high income flow and little net wealth because it spends heavily or owes large debts. Another can have modest current income and substantial wealth from prior savings or asset ownership. Similarly, a government's annual deficit and its outstanding debt measure different objects. Reconcile the opening and closing stock A stock changes through transactions and other changes during the period. A general reconciliation is: Closing stock = opening stock + additions − reductions + other changes For physical inventory, additions can be purchases or production; reductions can be sales, use or spoilage. For cash, additions and reductions are cash receipts and payments. For the measured value of an asset, other changes can include price movements even when no transaction occurs. The categories depend on what the stock measures. Interest capitalized into a loan increases outstanding debt. A payment reduces the obligation according to the contract's allocation rules. When all interest and borrowing are added to a modeled balance and all payments are subtracted from that same balance, the reconciliation must preserve those conventions consistently. If a stock does not reconcile, possibilities include a missing transaction, a valuation change, inconsistent reporting dates, mismatched units, or an error. Identify the difference rather than hiding it in an unexplained “other” category. A reconciliation is an analytical check and often the start of an investigation. Worked example 1 — Reconcile a loan balance A simplified credit balance is $1,000 at the start of a month. During the month, $300 of new borrowing and $20 of interest are added. The borrower makes a $150 payment. There are no fees, write-offs or other changes. Closing debt = 1,000 + 300 + 20 − 150 = $1,170 Debt rises by $170 despite the payment because additions exceed the reduction. The $150 payment is a flow; the $1,170 closing balance is a stock. The payment cannot be equated with a $150 improvement in the household's net wealth without examining the cash used, the interest expense and the other transactions. If the closing statement instead shows $1,190, there is a $20 unexplained difference. Check for a fee, an omitted purchase, timing differences or an incorrect record. A correct analyst does not silently force the reported balance to match by inventing a transaction. Assets, liabilities and equity An asset is a resource or claim held by an entity. A liability is an obligation of that entity. Equity , or net worth in a simplified household balance sheet, is the residual after liabilities are subtracted from assets. The core identity is: Assets = liabilities + equity Equity = assets − liabilities The identity does not say that equity can always be realized at its recorded amount. Asset values may be uncertain; selling can incur costs; liabilities may have contractual and legal complexities. Later accounting and valuation chapters examine measurement. For now, use a consistent specified basis and reporting date. Borrowing money increases cash and liabilities by the same amount at origination in a simple transaction. It does not by itself create income or equity. Spending cash on an asset exchanges one asset for another. Paying down loan principal reduces cash and debt equally. Earning profit can increase equity, while an owner's capital contribution increases equity without being revenue. These distinctions prevent serious errors in business and policy reasoning. Cash inflow is not synonymous with revenue. Cash outflow is not synonymous with expense. A transaction's classification depends on what happened economically and on the specified accounting rules. Profit and cash flow Profit summarizes revenues minus recognized expenses for a period. Cash flow summarizes receipts and payments. Under accrual accounting, revenue can be recorded before a customer pays, and expenses can reflect resources consumed at a different time from the related cash payment. A company that sells $100 of goods on credit can record revenue while receiving no immediate cash. It holds a receivable instead. Purchasing a durable machine is an immediate cash outflow, but its cost is generally allocated across periods through depreciation under the applicable accounting method. A business can therefore be profitable while facing a cash shortage. Likewise, raising a loan can improve cash liquidity while leaving profit unchanged at origination. Interest affects later earnings and cash according to timing; repaying principal reduces the obligation. Keep the income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow statement distinct, then reconcile their connections. Worked example 2 — A simple business from transactions to statements An owner contributes $1,000 cash. The business borrows $600, purchases equipment for $500 cash, purchases inventory for $300 cash, and sells all that inventory for $450 cash. Assume no depreciation, taxes, interest, fees or other transactions during this short illustrative period. Transaction Cash change Other effect Owner contribution +$1,000 Equity +$1,000 Loan received +$600 Debt +$600 Equipment bought −$500 Equipment +$500 Inventory bought −$300 Inventory +$300 Inventory sold +$450 Inventory −$300; profit +$150 Ending cash is 1,000 + 600 − 500 − 300 + 450 = $1,250. Profit is sales revenue $450 minus inventory cost consumed $300 = $150. Assets are cash $1,250 plus equipment $500 = $1,750. Liabilities are $600. Equity is the contribution $1,000 plus retained profit $150 = $1,150. The balance sheet reconciles: 1,750 = 600 + 1,150. Cash-flow categories also reconcile: operating cash flow is 450 − 300 = $150; investing cash flow is −$500; financing cash flow is 1,000 + 600 = $1,600. Their sum is $1,250. The same final cash appears through a different organized view of the transactions. The example's excluded expenses are explicit assumptions, not a claim that real companies avoid depreciation, interest or tax. Including those consequences would change the statements and must be done consistently. The same claim has two sides A financial claim can be an asset for one party and a liability for another. A bank deposit is an asset of the depositor and an obligation of the bank. A loan is an asset of the lender and a liability of the borrower. When combining entities' balance sheets, do not count an internal claim as an external net asset of the combined group. Consider two entities where A lends B $100. A records a $100 claim; B records a $100 liability. Consolidating them eliminates the internal claim and obligation. This does not mean the transaction has no significance: contracts affect incentives, liquidity and risk within the group. It means the consolidated group's external net financial position must not count both sides as new net wealth. For an economy, the interpretation becomes more complex because domestic financial claims, real assets and claims on foreign parties differ. National accounting formalizes those boundaries. The general habit remains valuable: specify the entity or group, the date, the valuation convention and whether the claim is internal or external. Build an auditable reconciliation Begin with an opening statement whose identity balances. Record each transaction with effects on both assets and financing claims. Distinguish a transaction from a valuation change and from an estimate. Recompute the closing statement and explain any difference. Keep the original records so another analyst can follow the steps. A spreadsheet that balances only because an unexplained financing item absorbs every mistake is unreliable. Some forecasting models intentionally use a specified funding policy to close a cash gap; that is acceptable when the policy is explicit. An unexplained balancing plug is not equivalent to a realistic financing decision. Exercises Classify each quantity as a stock or flow and give a suitable time description: outstanding mortgage debt, annual wages, cash on hand, quarterly equipment purchases, inventory, and interest paid during a month. Inventory begins at 80 units. The business buys 50 units, sells 90 and loses 5 to spoilage. Find closing inventory. If the count instead shows 40, calculate the unexplained difference. A household has cash of $2,000, investments valued at $8,000 and debt of $3,000. Find equity. Then show the balance-sheet effects of borrowing another $1,000 and keeping the cash. Using the household after the borrowing in Exercise 3, repay $500 of loan principal with cash. Show closing assets, debt and equity. Explain why the principal payment is not by itself a $500 loss of net worth. A business earns $1,200 of revenue from a sale on credit but receives no cash yet. The sold inventory cost $700 and was already paid for. Ignoring all other expenses, what happens to receivables, inventory, profit, equity and current cash? A business's opening cash is $100. During the period it collects $900 from customers, pays $600 of operating costs, buys $200 of equipment, borrows $300 and repays $150 of principal. Calculate operating, investing and financing cash flows and closing cash. A shareholding is valued at $5,000 at the beginning of a period and $5,400 at the end. There are no purchases, sales or dividends. Explain the $400 stock change and why it is not a transaction inflow to cash. Entity A has $500 cash and a $200 loan claim on B. Entity B has $300 cash and owes A $200. There are no other assets or liabilities. Calculate each entity's net worth and the consolidated group's cash, external liabilities and net worth. Explain the elimination. Solutions and evaluation criteria Mortgage debt, cash and inventory are stocks at a date. Wages, purchases and interest payments are flows over intervals. The units and reporting dates must match the classification. Closing inventory is 80 + 50 − 90 − 5 = 35 units. A count of 40 exceeds the reconciliation by 5. Investigate records, timing and counting rather than assuming an unrecorded purchase. Opening equity is 2,000 + 8,000 − 3,000 = $7,000. Borrowing increases cash to $3,000 and debt to $4,000, leaving assets of $11,000 and equity of $7,000. Cash becomes $2,500, investments remain $8,000 and debt becomes $3,500. Assets are $10,500 and equity remains $7,000. Cash and principal owed decline together. Interest, fees or other consequences would require additional entries. Receivables rise $1,200; inventory falls $700. Profit and equity rise $500. Current cash does not change on this credit sale. Future collection converts the receivable into cash without recording the sale a second time. Operating flow is 900 − 600 = $300. Investing flow is −$200. Financing flow is 300 − 150 = $150. Net cash change is $250; closing cash is $350. Borrowing is financing, not operating revenue. The holding has a $400 valuation increase. It changes measured asset value and, under an appropriately specified measurement basis, net worth; it does not add cash. Accounting recognition of the gain depends on the applicable classification and rules. A's net worth is $700. B's is $300 − $200 = $100. Combined cash is $800; there is no external liability in the stated case. Consolidated net worth is $800. Remove A's internal $200 claim and B's matching $200 obligation; summing the individual net worths also gives $800. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why receiving loan proceeds can increase cash without increasing profit or net worth, and why earning revenue can increase profit without immediately increasing cash. Delayed check: record five original business transactions and reconstruct their effects on cash, other assets, liabilities and equity. The closing identity must balance without an unexplained plug. Where this leads Financial accounting develops formal recognition and measurement. Corporate finance converts forecasts into value. Macroeconomics connects income flows, capital stocks and sector balances. Banking and crises require balance-sheet reasoning because financing claims transmit obligations, liquidity demands and losses across institutions."}, {"title": "01.05 · Compounding and present value", "url": "v01-c05.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Put cash flows on the same date A dollar available today and a dollar promised a year from now differ in timing, risk and purchasing power. To compare choices, specify when each payment occurs and bring the payments to one valuation date. Start with a simple setting: certain cash flows, a constant per-period rate, and a feasible opportunity to borrow or invest at that rate without fees or restrictions. These assumptions make the timing arithmetic transparent. Real decisions may require different rates, credit adjustments, taxes, currency conventions or borrowing constraints. Later finance chapters develop those issues. A correct present-value calculation still needs an economically justified discount rate. Let V 0 be an amount at time 0 and r the rate per period expressed as a decimal. If interest stays invested, the amount after one period is V 0 (1 + r). After two periods, interest also accrues on the previous period's interest. Repeating the rule gives: Future value after n periods = V₀(1 + r)ⁿ Specify what a period means. If r is a monthly rate, n counts months. If r is an annual rate, n counts years. A rate of 5% enters as 0.05. For standard positive discount factors, require 1 + r > 0. Negative rates above −100% are mathematically possible, although their economic interpretation and feasibility must be examined. Compounding and effective rates Simple interest on the original principal alone yields V 0 (1 + nr). Compound interest yields V 0 (1 + r) n . At $100 and 10% per year for two years, simple interest produces $120; annual compounding produces $121. The additional dollar reflects interest earned on the first year's interest. If a nominal annual rate j is compounded m times per year, the specified periodic rate is j/m and its effective annual rate is (1 + j/m) m − 1. That is a rate convention, not a universal interpretation of any quoted number. A 12% nominal rate compounded monthly gives a 1% monthly rate and approximately 12.68% effective annual growth. Fees, interest-calculation conventions and cash-flow timing can make a quoted rate differ from an all-in borrowing cost. Read the specified terms and calculate using the actual payments. Do not assume an illustrative mathematical rate equals a jurisdiction's legally defined disclosure measure. Discounting reverses compounding If an amount C is available n periods from now, the present value is the amount that would grow to C under the specified rate: Present value = C / (1 + r)ⁿ At a 10% annual rate, $1,000 due in two years has a present value of 1,000/1.1 2 , approximately $826.45. Check by compounding the unrounded result forward two years. A higher positive discount rate gives a lower present value for the same positive future payment because less initial money is needed to grow to that payment. For several dated cash flows, discount each to the same date and add: Present value = Σ Cₜ / (1 + r)ᵗ Net present value = −initial cost + present value of later net cash flows The symbol Σ means “sum over the listed periods.” Use net cash flows that include the relevant benefits and costs. Do not subtract an expense again if it was already deducted in constructing a net cash flow. Cash at time 0 has no period of discounting: (1 + r) 0 = 1. Worked example 1 — An investment with two payments A project requires $1,000 now and pays $600 at the end of each of the next two years. Assume these are certain net cash flows, the opportunity rate is 10% annually, and there are no other consequences. Time-0 cash flow is −$1,000. The first payment's present value is 600/1.1 = $545.4545…. The second payment's present value is 600/1.1 2 = $495.8678…. NPV is −1,000 + 545.4545… + 495.8678… = $41.3223…, displayed as $41.32. The positive NPV means the project exceeds the specified feasible opportunity in this setting. Adding $600 + $600 − $1,000 gives $200, but ignores timing. Using the same unrounded amounts, the project's advantage at year 2 is 600 × 1.1 + 600 − 1,000 × 1.1 2 = $50. Discounting $50 back two years gives the same $41.32 NPV. If the opportunity rate is 20%, NPV becomes −1,000 + 600/1.2 + 600/1.2 2 = −$83.33 approximately. A decision can change when an important assumption changes. Uncertain project payments would require a risk analysis rather than being labeled certain for convenience. Annuities and repeated payments An ordinary annuity pays the same amount A at the end of each period for n periods. Let d = 1/(1 + r). Its present value is A(d + d 2 + … + d n ). Multiplying the sum by d shifts every term one period. Subtracting the shifted sum from the original cancels the middle terms: S = d + d² + … + dⁿ dS = d² + d³ + … + dⁿ⁺¹ (1 − d)S = d − dⁿ⁺¹ For r ≠ 0, rearranging yields the annuity formula: PV = A × [1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ] / r If r = 0, use PV = nA directly. An annuity paid at the beginning of each period is an annuity due; with identical rates and n payments, it has the ordinary annuity's value multiplied by (1 + r), because each payment arrives one period earlier. Draw the timeline before choosing a formula. A perpetual constant end-of-period payment A has PV = A/r if r > 0 and the payments truly continue indefinitely. This mathematical limit is useful, but a finite business, contract or investment need not actually pay forever. Terminal-value models must justify their assumptions. Worked example 2 — A level-payment loan A loan advances $1,200 now. It has a 1% monthly rate and is repaid through 12 equal end-of-month payments, with no fees or additional charges. The present value of those payments must equal the advance. 1,200 = A × [1 − 1.01⁻¹²] / 0.01 A = 1,200 × 0.01 / [1 − 1.01⁻¹²] A ≈ $106.62 per month Using the unrounded payment, total payments are approximately $1,279.42. The difference from the $1,200 advance is approximately $79.42. Contractual rounding can make the final payment slightly different. The first month's interest is $1,200 × 0.01 = $12. The principal reduction is approximately 106.6185 − 12 = $94.6185, leaving approximately $1,105.3815. As principal declines, the interest portion declines and the principal portion rises if the payment and rate remain constant. The loan recurrence is B t = B t−1 (1 + r) − A. Applying it for 12 periods with the unrounded payment produces a final balance of zero up to numerical precision. This independent check tests the formula against the actual payment schedule. Nominal and real cash flows A nominal amount is expressed in the money units of its date. A real amount is adjusted to a specified purchasing-power basis. If a nominal investment return is r and the applicable price index grows at rate π over the same period, the purchasing-power growth factor is (1 + r)/(1 + π). The exact real return is that factor minus one. A 10% nominal gain with 5% inflation gives 1.10/1.05 − 1, approximately 4.76% real growth, rather than exactly 5%. Subtracting inflation from the nominal return is an approximation that can be useful for small rates. It is not the exact relation. Chapter 7 examines the measurement and interpretation of price indices. Use nominal cash flows with nominal rates and real cash flows with corresponding real rates. Consistency also applies to currency, tax treatment and compounding frequency. A technically correct formula with incompatible inputs produces an economically incorrect comparison. What present value does not decide by itself The calculation does not identify the correct cash-flow forecast, measure every nonmonetary consequence, or determine which risk-adjusted rate fits a real investment. A borrower who cannot finance an attractive project faces a constraint absent from the simple example. Liquidity, default risk and the distribution of outcomes can matter even when expected NPV is positive. Use the timeline and formulas to make assumptions visible. Then ask what could change: payment dates, inflation, reinvestment opportunities, borrowing costs, risk, fees and feasible alternatives. The value of discounting is that it disciplines comparison rather than making uncertainty disappear. Solving for an unknown rate can require a nonlinear equation. For the two-payment loan in Exercise 8, substituting x = 1 + r produces a quadratic. For ax 2 + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, the quadratic formula is x = [−b ± √(b 2 − 4ac)]/(2a). The expression under the square root must be nonnegative for real roots. Check candidates in the original equation and retain only values satisfying the model's domain, including x > 0 for a positive discount factor. Exercises Calculate the future value of $500 after three annual periods at 8% compounded annually. Compare it with simple interest at 8% over the same horizon. Find the present value of $2,000 due three years from now at a 5% annual rate. Verify the answer by compounding it forward. A project costs $900 now and pays $500 at the end of each of the next two years. At a 10% rate, calculate NPV and state the conditional decision. Calculate the present value of three end-of-year payments of $200 at 10% both by summing the payments and by the annuity formula. What is the value if the rate is zero? A contract promises three $100 payments: one immediately, one in a year and one in two years. Calculate its value at a 10% annual rate. Explain why the ordinary three-period annuity formula alone has the wrong timing. A nominal 6% annual rate is compounded monthly. Find the monthly rate and effective annual rate. State why this calculation alone does not measure the cost of a loan that also has fees. An investment gains 8% nominally while the applicable price index rises 3%. Calculate the exact real return and compare it with the subtraction approximation. A loan advances $1,000 now and requires two end-of-year payments of $600. Derive the equation for the effective annual financing rate, express it as a quadratic in x = 1 + r, and solve for the economically admissible positive x. Ignore fees and default. Solutions and evaluation criteria Compound value is 500 × 1.08 3 = $629.856, or $629.86. Simple-interest value is 500 × (1 + 3 × 0.08) = $620. The $9.856 difference comes from compounding. PV = 2,000/1.05 3 = $1,727.675… approximately $1,727.68. Multiplying the unrounded value by 1.05 3 restores $2,000. NPV = −900 + 500/1.1 + 500/1.1 2 = −$32.2314… approximately −$32.23. Under the stated opportunity rate, certain payments and complete consequences, reject the project in favor of the specified alternative. The sum is 200/1.1 + 200/1.1 2 + 200/1.1 3 = $497.370… approximately $497.37. The annuity expression gives the same answer. At zero interest, PV is 3 × 200 = $600. PV = 100 + 100/1.1 + 100/1.1 2 = $273.5537… approximately $273.55. The payments occur at times 0,1,2 rather than 1,2,3. It is a three-payment annuity due. The monthly rate is 0.06/12 = 0.005, or 0.5%. Effective annual growth is 1.005 12 − 1, approximately 6.17%. Fees alter the borrower's actual advance or payments, so calculate from the full cash-flow schedule. The exact return is 1.08/1.03 − 1, approximately 4.85%. Subtraction gives 5%; it is an approximation. 1,000 = 600/x + 600/x 2 , hence 1,000x 2 − 600x − 600 = 0. The positive root is x = (600 + √2,760,000)/2,000 ≈ 1.130662386. Thus r ≈ 13.0662%. The negative root is inadmissible for the positive discount-factor convention. Substitution verifies the two payments' present value equals the advance. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: draw a three-payment timeline and explain why moving a payment one period earlier changes its present value. Derive the ordinary annuity formula by showing which terms cancel. Delayed check: construct a loan balance schedule using the recurrence, verify its final balance, and explain the difference between a quoted periodic rate and an all-in cost based on actual payments. Where this leads Corporate finance evaluates project and business cash flows. Bond valuation discounts contractual payments using a term structure. Asset pricing determines how uncertain payments are valued across states. The simple formulas here establish timing discipline; advanced valuation adds the economics of risk, information and market constraints."}, {"title": "01.06 · Incentives, marginal decisions and equilibrium", "url": "v01-c06.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Actions respond to consequences An incentive changes the consequences of an action. A higher wage can make working an additional hour more attractive. A price increase can make conserving a good more attractive. A penalty for late delivery can encourage timeliness. Predicting the response requires knowing the alternatives, constraints, information and objectives; the existence of an incentive does not determine its magnitude automatically. A person can respond on several margins. A consumer facing a higher fuel price may drive less, buy a more efficient vehicle, move closer to work, or reduce other spending. Those responses take different amounts of time and may be constrained. A short-run response need not equal a long-run response. Incentives can also change reporting instead of the underlying activity. Paying employees for a measured target may encourage real improvement, manipulation of the measurement, or neglect of unmeasured tasks. Good analysis asks what actions are rewarded and what the metric fails to capture. Private choice and social consequences A privately attractive action may impose costs on others. A firm may gain by producing more while its emissions affect neighboring residents. Conversely, a person who improves public health can create benefits beyond their own. These consequences are called external effects when they are not fully accounted for through the relevant decision's prices or arrangements. Separate the decision maker's calculation from the evaluation of everyone's consequences. To predict behavior, ask what the actor faces. To evaluate a policy, specify whose outcomes count, the feasible alternatives and any distributional concerns. A statement that an actor optimized does not prove that the outcome is socially desirable. Rules and institutions shape the feasible choices. Property rights, contracts, enforcement, professional norms and information systems can change the incentive structure. When a policy fails, examine the mechanism and implementation rather than assuming that a stated intention became the actual incentive. Optimization and equilibrium are different ideas Optimization describes an actor choosing the best feasible action under a specified objective. Equilibrium describes a set of choices or prices that are mutually consistent under a model. A competitive market model often requires each buyer and seller to choose appropriately given prices and the quantities supplied and demanded to balance. A point where supply equals demand is a market-clearing condition. It does not show by itself that choices are optimal, that adjustment reaches the point, or that the outcome is fair. A game-theoretic equilibrium uses a different consistency condition: strategies must be best responses to one another. Later chapters formalize both concepts. A model can have multiple equilibria or none under its assumptions. Equilibrium is an analytical concept; observed markets can contain rationing, queues, inventories, delayed contracts or price rules. Use a model's conditions to examine reality rather than assuming reality must satisfy every condition. Worked example 1 — A marginal production decision A producer can sell additional units for $20 each and make up to four additional units. Their incremental costs are $8, $12, $18 and $25. Assume there are no other consequences, each unit can be chosen sequentially, and these costs include all relevant opportunities. The incremental net benefits are 12, 8, 2 and −5 dollars. Producing three units adds $22 of value. Producing a fourth reduces total value to $17. If price falls to $15, the incremental net benefits become 7, 3, −3 and −10. Producing two units is best, adding $10. The response follows a margin: the third unit becomes unattractive when its $18 cost exceeds the new $15 benefit. A historical fixed cost already incurred does not change this additional-unit comparison unless it implies a distinct future consequence. Capacity also matters: even a higher price cannot create a fifth unit if the technology permits only four. Movements along a curve and shifts of a curve A demand relationship specifies quantities buyers would choose at different prices while other specified influences are held fixed. Changing price under that fixed relationship produces a movement along the curve. Changing income, preferences, expectations or another relevant input can change the relationship itself, producing a shift. A supply relationship similarly connects price and offered quantity while fixing technology, input costs and other relevant conditions. Cheaper inputs can shift supply even if the final product's price has not changed. To analyze a new equilibrium, identify which relationship changed, write the new rule and solve the consistency condition. These are conditional statements, not universal signs. A simple demand curve often slopes downward; exceptions and aggregation complications require more careful models. Holding other variables fixed is especially important when observed price and quantity both change because their equilibrium responds to several forces. Worked example 2 — A per-unit tax creates a wedge Buyers demand Q d = 120 − 2P b ; sellers supply Q s = 20 + 3P s . P b is the buyer's price and P s is the seller's receipt. Without tax, both equal $20 and quantity is 80. A $4 per-unit tax creates P b = P s + 4. 120 − 2(Pₛ + 4) = 20 + 3Pₛ 112 − 2Pₛ = 20 + 3Pₛ Pₛ = 92 / 5 = $18.40 Pᵦ = $22.40 Q = 20 + 3(18.40) = 75.20 Relative to the original price, buyers pay $2.40 more and sellers receive $1.60 less. Buyers bear 60% and sellers 40% of the $4 price wedge in this model. Statutory remittance—the party legally sending the tax payment—does not alone determine economic incidence. Tax receipts are 4 × 75.2 = $300.80 per modeled period. That revenue is not itself a complete welfare calculation; some effects are transfers, while changed quantities and external effects require additional analysis. The model excludes evasion, collection costs, market power and effects in other markets. Its fractional quantity is a continuous approximation. Adjustment and institutions If price is below a model's market-clearing price, quantity demanded can exceed quantity supplied. Calling this excess demand is a statement about the model's planned quantities. It does not tell you which buyer receives the available good. Queues, priority rules, lotteries, contracts or other mechanisms can allocate the supply. If price is above the clearing value, planned supply can exceed demand. Sellers might accumulate inventory or revise future production. Whether they cut prices depends on contracts, coordination, information and the relevant market structure. An explicit adjustment rule is needed to make a prediction about speed or stability. A price floor or ceiling changes feasible prices under a policy. If a ceiling lies below the unconstrained clearing price and the simple curves remain applicable, excess demand arises. The policy's actual effect depends on enforcement, quality changes, rationing and other responses. Describe these margins rather than stopping after naming a shortage. Elasticity measures responsiveness A slope measures change in units; an elasticity measures a proportional response. For a finite comparison from a positive base, a simple elasticity calculation divides the percentage change in quantity by the percentage change in price. The base convention must be stated. Later calculus gives point elasticity, and midpoint formulas offer a symmetric finite-change convention. Elasticity is useful because units such as dollars versus cents no longer change its value when the ratio is constructed consistently. It still depends on the starting point, range, time horizon and which other influences are held fixed. “Demand is inelastic” is incomplete without explaining where and over what horizon. In the tax model, the relatively more responsive supply side adjusts quantity more readily and bears less of the price wedge. This is a mechanism to analyze under stated competitive conditions. Incidence in settings with market power, incomplete contracts or dynamic responses can require a different model. Evaluate claims by tracing a mechanism For an incentive claim, identify the affected action, the changed consequence and the constraint. For an equilibrium claim, identify the consistency conditions. For a welfare claim, identify whose consequences are included. Then ask what evidence would support the proposed mechanism or distinguish it from alternatives. A policy can achieve one goal while creating another problem. That is a reason to compare the full consequences and feasible alternatives, not a reason to assume every intervention fails. Transparent analysis states trade-offs, uncertainty and the assumptions on which a recommendation rests. Exercises A producer sells at $12 per unit. The first four incremental-unit costs are $4, $8, $11 and $15. Find the best additional output and total incremental net benefit. Repeat at a $9 price. Demand is Q d = 100 − 2P and supply is Q s = 10 + P. Find the equilibrium. Then income changes demand to 130 − 2P while supply stays fixed. Find the new equilibrium and distinguish the shift from the movement along the unchanged supply curve. Using the original demand and supply from Exercise 2, impose a $6 per-unit tax with P b = P s + 6. Find buyer price, seller price, quantity, buyer and seller shares of the price wedge, and tax receipts. Using the original untaxed model in Exercise 2, set a binding price ceiling of $20. Find planned demand, planned supply and excess demand. Explain why the model does not identify who gets the goods. Price rises from $10 to $12 while quantity demanded falls from 50 to 45 along a fixed relationship. Calculate the percentage changes using the original bases and their ratio. Why would a different finite-change convention need to be stated? A school rewards teachers for a single test score. Describe a response that improves learning and a response that improves the score while harming another educational objective. Identify the relevant measurement limitation. A firm chooses output optimally given prices, but its emissions harm neighbors and are unpriced. Explain why private optimization alone does not establish social efficiency. Identify what a policy comparison would need to include. A commentator observes price and quantity increasing together and concludes that demand curves slope upward. Give a demand-shift explanation consistent with downward-sloping demand. State what additional information is needed to estimate a demand response. Solutions and evaluation criteria At $12, net increments are 8,4,1,−3; produce three for $13. At $9, increments are 5,1,−2,−6; produce two for $6. Compare the cumulative alternatives as a check. 100 − 2P = 10 + P gives P = 30 and Q = 40. After the demand shift, 130 − 2P = 10 + P gives P = 40 and Q = 50. Demand's rule changed; suppliers move along their unchanged relationship as price rises. 100 − 2(P s + 6) = 10 + P s gives P s = 26, P b = 32 and Q = 36. Buyers pay $2 more than 30 and sellers receive $4 less, giving one-third and two-thirds of the $6 wedge. Tax receipts are 6 × 36 = $216. At 20, planned demand is 60 and supply is 30, leaving excess demand of 30. Allocation requires a rationing or trading institution; the two equations alone do not specify it. Price rises 20%; quantity falls 10%; the ratio is −0.5. Original-base and midpoint conventions can differ for finite changes, so an analyst must identify the convention. The ratio describes the stipulated fixed relationship, not an arbitrary observational association. Teachers may improve comprehension through better instruction. They may also narrow instruction to tested items or manipulate reporting. Evaluate actual learning across relevant objectives, not only the rewarded measure. Several coherent mechanisms are acceptable. The firm compares private benefits and costs while omitting the neighbors' harm. A policy analysis must consider that harm, changes in production and consumption, implementation costs, distribution, and feasible alternatives. Private optimality under incomplete prices is insufficient to establish efficiency. An outward demand shift can raise both clearing price and quantity while each demand curve slopes downward. Estimating movement along demand requires separating supply-driven price variation from demand shifts and addressing confounding. Observation of equilibrium changes alone cannot identify the curve. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the difference between an actor's optimum, market clearing, and a socially desirable outcome. Use a tax wedge to explain why the remitting party need not bear the whole economic burden. Delayed check: invent a supply-demand model with a price rule or tax, solve its conditions, and describe an institutional detail that could change the real-world response. Where this leads Microeconomics formalizes preferences, technology, competition and strategic behavior. Public economics analyzes incidence, externalities and distribution. Institutional analysis examines implementation. Econometrics asks whether a mechanism is supported by credible evidence. Incentive and equilibrium reasoning connects those fields."}, {"title": "01.07 · Prices, inflation and purchasing power", "url": "v01-c07.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Separate money amounts from purchasing power A higher salary does not necessarily buy more goods and services. If money income rises by 5% while the prices relevant to a household rise by 8%, the household's purchasing power falls. To analyze this, distinguish a nominal amount, a price level or index, and a real amount expressed on a specified purchasing-power basis. The price of a good is the money required per unit of that good. A relative price compares its price with another price. An economy contains many prices, so a general price index summarizes selected movements using specified items and weights. It is a measurement construction, not a price that every household literally pays. Inflation is the percentage increase in a specified price index over a specified interval. State which index, which population or activity it represents, the interval, and whether the comparison uses adjusted or unadjusted values. A statement that “prices rose 4%” leaves important measurement choices unstated. Construct a simple fixed-basket index Choose a basket of quantities q i,0 in a base period, and price that same basket in each comparison period. Let p i,t be the price of item i in period t. An illustrative fixed-basket index is: Basket cost at time t = Σ pᵢ,ₜ qᵢ,₀ Price index Iₜ = 100 × basket cost at t / basket cost in the base period The base period's index is 100 by construction. An index of 120 means the specified basket costs 20% more than in the base period. It does not mean that every item rose 20%, that every household's expenditures rose 20%, or that inflation during the latest period was 20%. Inflation between two dates uses their ratio: Inflation from s to t = Iₜ / Iₛ − 1 If an index moves from 120 to 126, inflation is 126/120 − 1 = 5%, not 6%. The six-index-point increase is a difference in index levels. The preceding chapter's denominator discipline applies directly. Worked example 1 — Price a basket and deflate income A hypothetical basket contains two loaves of bread and three transit trips. In the base period, bread costs $2 and a trip costs $10. Later, bread costs $3 and a trip costs $11. These are illustrative prices, not observations about October 2026. Base basket cost is 2 × 2 + 3 × 10 = $34. Later basket cost is 2 × 3 + 3 × 11 = $39. The later index is 100 × 39/34 ≈ 114.71. Basket inflation is 39/34 − 1 ≈ 14.71%. If nominal income rises from $3,400 to $3,900, it buys 100 baskets in each period. Purchasing power relative to this basket is unchanged. Bread rises 50%, while trips rise 10%. The basket inflation is neither the unweighted mean of those rates nor a statement that every person experiences the same change. Base expenditure weights are 4/34 for bread and 30/34 for trips. Their weighted price changes reproduce the basket's 5/34 increase. Deflate a nominal amount To express a nominal amount N t in the base period's purchasing power when the index is based at 100, divide by I t /100: Real amount in base-period money = Nₜ / (Iₜ / 100) More generally, to express an amount at time t in the price basis of period b, multiply by I b /I t . An income of $63,000 when the index is 126 equals $50,000 in the price basis of an index at 100. The calculation is conditional on the chosen index being appropriate for the question. For growth between two dates, use nominal growth factor divided by the price growth factor. A nominal wage increase of 6% with 8% inflation produces a real growth factor of 1.06/1.08, or approximately −1.85% real growth. Simply subtracting the rates gives a −2% approximation. Comparisons also need consistent populations and hours. An increase in average annual earnings can reflect more hours or a changing worker composition. A real hourly wage for comparable work answers a different question from average annual nominal earnings across a changing population. Worked example 2 — A wage gain with a purchasing-power loss A worker earns $50,000 at time 0 and $53,000 at time 1. The relevant illustrative price index moves from 100 to 108. Nominal wage growth is 53,000/50,000 − 1 = 6%. Price growth is 108/100 − 1 = 8%. The later wage in time-0 purchasing power is 53,000/1.08 ≈ $49,074.07. Real wage growth is 1.06/1.08 − 1 ≈ −1.85%. The conclusion concerns purchasing power under the chosen basket and assumes comparable work. If the worker's expenditure basket differs substantially, another index could be more informative. Changes in taxes, household size or working hours require separate analysis rather than being silently attributed to inflation. Inflation, disinflation and deflation Inflation describes a rising price index. Disinflation describes a reduction in its positive rate of increase: for example, annual inflation dropping from 8% to 3%. The index can continue rising. Deflation describes a fall in the index, meaning a negative inflation rate over the relevant interval. If an index starts at 100, rises 8%, then rises 3%, it becomes 100 × 1.08 × 1.03 = 111.24. Inflation slowed, but the final price level is 11.24% above the starting level. A slowing increase is different from a reversal. A single good becoming cheaper is not necessarily general deflation. Likewise, an increase in one highly visible price does not establish the inflation rate of a broad basket. Relative price changes can be large even when the general price level changes little. Measurement limits and appropriate indices An index must define the population, items, weights, measurement timing and treatment of changing products. A fixed basket is transparent, but people can substitute between goods when relative prices change. Quality can improve or deteriorate; new goods appear; the relevant basket can evolve. A complete cost-of-living comparison asks how much spending would maintain a specified level of wellbeing, which is more demanding than repricing an unchanged basket. The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics explains that CPI averages need not match an individual household's experience and that its index is not a complete measure of every factor affecting living standards. Use its documentation for the actual index's scope and methodology. Our two-item calculations are teaching models, not replicas of its full statistical process. BLS CPI documentation . Consumer-price, producer-price and output-price indices answer different questions. A measure designed for household consumption is not automatically the best deflator for a firm's output or a national production aggregate. Choose the index for the conceptual quantity being analyzed and document the match. Index bases and comparisons Rebasing an index changes its scale without changing proportional movements. If the current series is 100, 110 and 121, rebasing the middle date to 100 produces 90.909…, 100 and 110. Both series imply a 10% increase in each interval. Differences in displayed index levels across series do not establish differences in their current purchasing power. Comparing places is another question. An inflation index tracks changes through time within its defined setting; it does not by itself provide a cross-country price-level comparison. Exchange rates convert currencies but can differ from purchasing-power comparisons. Later international economics develops those distinctions. When comparing data releases, distinguish the reference period from the release date. Some series are revised or seasonally adjusted. Record the exact version used in an analysis. This chapter makes no claim about the latest inflation reading; it teaches how to interpret a documented series. Why this matters for decisions Real and nominal consistency affects wages, budgets, investment returns and contract values. A fixed nominal payment can lose purchasing power when prices rise. A nominal debt obligation and a variable-rate debt contract respond differently to inflation and rate changes. The effects also depend on expectations and contractual terms, so a general statement that “inflation helps borrowers” needs qualifications. For analysis, state the price basis of every real number, keep the time period consistent, and preserve the distinction between money gains and purchasing-power gains. The arithmetic is simple enough to audit independently. The harder step is choosing the appropriate economic measure. Exercises A fixed basket contains four units of A and one unit of B. Base prices are $5 and $20; later prices are $6 and $22. Calculate both basket costs, the later base-100 index and inflation. An index rises from 125 to 130. Calculate the index-point increase and the percentage change. Explain why they differ. A salary is $60,000 when the price index is 120. Express it in the purchasing-power basis where the index is 100. If nominal salary rises to $63,000 while the index rises to 126, calculate real salary growth. An asset gains 12% nominally over a period in which the relevant price index rises 7%. Calculate its exact real return. A price index starts at 100 and rises 10%, then 2%. Find the final level. Explain whether the second period has inflation, disinflation or deflation. Rebase the index series 80, 100, 120 so that the first period equals 100. Show that the final-to-middle percentage change is unchanged. A household spends 80% of its base budget on category X and 20% on Y. X prices rise 5% and Y prices rise 20%, with the fixed basket otherwise unchanged. Calculate its basket inflation. Compare with a household whose weights are reversed. A report concludes that every household became 4% worse off because a consumer-price index rose 4%. Identify at least four missing pieces of information or conceptual problems. Give a more defensible statement. Solutions and evaluation criteria Base cost is 4 × 5 + 20 = $40. Later cost is 4 × 6 + 22 = $46. The index is 115 and inflation is 15%. The increase is 5 index points. Percentage growth is 5/125 = 4%. The index-point difference uses the scale; the percentage comparison uses the earlier level as denominator. The first salary is 60,000/1.20 = $50,000 in base purchasing power. The later one is 63,000/1.26 = $50,000. Real growth is zero: nominal salary and the index both rise 5%. Real return is 1.12/1.07 − 1 ≈ 4.67%. The five-percentage-point subtraction is only an approximation. The final index is 100 × 1.10 × 1.02 = 112.2. The second period has 2% inflation and disinflation relative to the earlier 10% rate. It does not have deflation. Multiply all values by 100/80 = 1.25 to obtain 100,125,150. The final-to-middle change is 120/100 − 1 = 20% in the original series and 150/125 − 1 = 20% in the rebased series. First basket inflation is 0.8 × 5% + 0.2 × 20% = 8%. Reversed weights produce 17%. This uses fixed quantities and base expenditure weights; the two households have different exposure. Needed information includes nominal income changes, household-specific expenditure patterns, taxes, household composition, relevant nonmarket outcomes and what “worse off” means. A defensible statement is that the specified index's measured price level rose 4% over the identified interval. Individual purchasing power and wellbeing require further analysis. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why inflation falling does not mean prices have fallen. Demonstrate with three index levels and distinguish a relative price from a general price index. Delayed check: construct a new fixed basket, compute its inflation, rebase its index, and deflate a nominal income without changing the underlying economic comparison. Where this leads Macroeconomics explains inflation mechanisms and policy. Statistics develops index measurement. Finance requires real-nominal consistency in valuation and returns. Distributional analysis asks how different households experience the same set of price changes. Accurate interpretation precedes any policy conclusion."}, {"title": "01.08 · Risk, uncertainty and evidence", "url": "v01-c08.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A decision can be good before its outcome is known Some choices have uncertain consequences. A business expansion can succeed or fail; a forecast can be wrong; an apparently safe receipt can arrive late. Evaluating a decision requires distinguishing the information available when the choice was made from the outcome that eventually occurred. A sound decision can lead to a disappointing realization, and a poorly reasoned gamble can happen to succeed. Evaluate the process using the probabilities, alternatives, constraints and information that were reasonably available at the decision time. Then use the realized outcome to improve future beliefs where it genuinely contains information. Define the outcomes before assigning probabilities. A scenario set should identify distinct possibilities and avoid overlap or omitted cases when it is presented as complete. “Revenue rises,” “revenue doubles,” and “revenue falls” are not three exclusive cases if doubling is included in rising. Make their definitions precise. Probabilities and expectations A probability lies between zero and one. Probabilities assigned to a complete set of mutually exclusive outcomes sum to one. Probabilities can arise from a specified chance mechanism, repeated observations under suitable conditions, or informed judgments. State their basis. Historical frequencies do not automatically apply to a changed environment. The expected value of a quantity is its probability-weighted average across outcomes: Expected value = Σ probability of outcome i × value in outcome i An expectation is not a guaranteed outcome and may not itself be one of the possible outcomes. If a project pays either $100 or −$50, an expected value of $55 does not mean the project literally pays $55 in a third scenario. Use expectations carefully. Expected revenue minus expected expense equals expected profit when the quantities are defined consistently, but a nonlinear function of an expectation generally differs from the expectation of that function. Later probability and decision theory formalize these distinctions. For now, compute each scenario's consequence first when the rule is nonlinear. Worked example 1 — Expected profit does not settle every decision Project A has net profit of $100 with probability 0.7 and a net loss of $50 with probability 0.3. Project B yields a certain net profit of $40. The choices are exclusive and occur over the same horizon. A's expected net profit is 0.7 × 100 + 0.3 × (−50) = $55. B's expected net profit is $40. A exceeds B by $15 in expected monetary profit. A still has a 30% probability of losing $50, while B has no loss in the specified model. An actor who maximizes expected monetary profit and can absorb the outcomes chooses A. An actor with a severe liquidity constraint or a strong preference for avoiding the loss may choose B. Neither preference can be inferred from the expectation alone. Specify the objective and financial capacity before making a recommendation. If A's success probability is p rather than a fixed 0.7, expected profit is 100p − 50(1 − p) = 150p − 50. It exceeds B's $40 when p > 0.6. The threshold shows which belief matters for the choice. Risk, uncertainty and ambiguity Usage differs across fields, but a useful distinction is between outcomes modeled with a specified probability distribution and uncertainty about the distribution itself. The latter is often called ambiguity. Knowing that profits can be +100 or −50 does not establish their probabilities. Where probabilities are poorly supported, present scenarios and sensitivity rather than assigning precision without evidence. For the project above, examine p across a plausible range. If all plausible values imply the same decision, the comparison is robust to that uncertainty. If the threshold lies inside the plausible range, additional information may be valuable. Different risks can interact. A household may lose income precisely when its investment performs badly. Treating the outcomes as independent can understate the bad combined scenario. Later covariance and dependence models quantify such relationships. At this stage, explicitly list which joint outcomes are feasible and why their probabilities are assigned. Downside and capacity to absorb loss Expected value averages across outcomes. It does not describe the worst case, the probability of a cash shortage, the size of losses when they occur, or whether an actor survives to benefit from favorable outcomes. A project with a high expected payoff can be infeasible if a required payment cannot be financed in a bad state. Describe the distribution and constraints relevant to the decision. Useful questions include: How much could be lost? When would cash be needed? Which obligations still fall due? What outcomes would violate a constraint? Can the actor delay, hedge, insure, reduce the position or obtain committed financing? The value of an outcome need not equal its money amount. Losing a small sum from a large buffer may have a different consequence from losing the same sum when it prevents essential spending. Expected-utility models later formalize preferences over uncertain outcomes. They do not eliminate the need to state whose objective and circumstances are being analyzed. Worked example 2 — Interpret a screening signal In an illustrative population of 10,000 applicants, 1% will default. A screening rule flags 90% of future defaulters and also flags 10% of applicants who will not default. Assume these rates apply to this population and that every applicant belongs to one of the two outcome groups. Actual outcome Applicants Flagged Not flagged Default 100 90 10 No default 9,900 990 8,910 Total 10,000 1,080 8,920 Among flagged applicants, 90/1,080 = 1/12 ≈ 8.33% default. The rule catches 90% of all defaults, but that does not mean 90% of flagged applicants default. The low original default frequency and the false-positive rate both matter. The example teaches conditional reasoning, not an actual credit decision. A lending policy would also need the costs of errors, repayment economics, legal constraints, fairness, measurement quality and whether the model transports to a new population. A signal's accuracy statistic alone cannot determine a responsible policy. Information can change a decision Information is valuable when it can improve the choice under the specified objective. Perfect information tells the relevant state before acting. Imperfect information changes beliefs without resolving all uncertainty. More information does not automatically justify a cost: it may not change the best action, or it may arrive after the decision. For an expected-money objective, compute expected payoff when decisions can respond to the signal, then subtract the best expected payoff without the signal. This is the gross value of information under the model. Its net value subtracts the cost of obtaining and using it. Later Bayesian decision theory develops the general calculation. Be careful about hindsight. Information acquired after a loss may explain what happened, but it could not guide a prior decision unless it was available then. Retrospective evaluations should reconstruct the information set at the decision time. Evidence and confidence Separate a claim from the observation offered to support it. An isolated success, an average from selected survivors, and a controlled comparison have different evidential value. Ask who was observed, who was omitted, how quantities were measured, and which alternative explanations remain possible. A large dataset can measure an association precisely while leaving its causal interpretation unresolved. A complicated forecast can be overfit. An impressive backtest can use information that was unavailable at the supposed decision time. Evidence needs a credible design and a transparent workflow, not only many observations or advanced terminology. Use uncertainty statements that identify what is uncertain: the outcome, the probability, the measured data, the causal effect, or the model itself. “We are uncertain” is less useful than explaining which assumption changes the conclusion and what evidence would resolve it. Exercises A project returns net profit of $200 with probability 0.4 and a net loss of $100 with probability 0.6. Calculate its expectation. Compare it with a certain $10 profit under an expected-money objective, and state a reason another objective could change the decision. A payoff is +$200 with probability p and −$100 otherwise. Find the success-probability threshold for positive expected profit. Evaluate the expectation at p = 0.3 and p = 0.5. Scenarios labeled “growth,” “growth above 10%,” and “decline” receive probabilities 0.5, 0.3 and 0.2. Explain why summing to one does not make them a valid exclusive scenario set. Rewrite categories that could be valid. In a population of 1,000, 10% have a specified condition. A signal detects 80% of those cases and falsely flags 10% of those without it. Find the probability of the condition among flagged cases using a count table. A loss of $100 occurs with probability 0.05; otherwise the loss is zero. Full insurance costs $6 and pays the $100 loss if it occurs. Calculate expected net monetary outcomes without and with insurance, ignoring all other consequences. Explain why the expected-money comparison does not decide every person's preference. A project yields +$80 in a favorable state with probability 0.6 and −$40 otherwise. Not undertaking it yields zero. Compute the best expected payoff without information, the expected payoff with perfect information before choosing, and the gross value of perfect information. You have only $30 available, no ability to borrow, and a project may require a $50 payment in a bad state. It has a positive expected profit. Explain why feasibility must be examined separately from expected profitability. A fund reports the performance of ten surviving strategies but excludes fifty discontinued strategies. Write a short critique identifying what evidence is missing, which inference is threatened, and what a better evaluation would require. Do not assume that all discontinued strategies lost money. Solutions and evaluation criteria The expectation is 0.4 × 200 − 0.6 × 100 = $20. It exceeds the certain $10 by $10 for the stated objective. A downside constraint or preference over risk can favor the certain alternative; identify the particular condition instead of asserting a universal preference. Expectation is 200p − 100(1 − p) = 300p − 100. It is positive for p > 1/3 and zero at p = 1/3. At 0.3 it is −$10; at 0.5 it is $50. “Growth above 10%” is included within “growth,” so the events overlap; unchanged output is also omitted unless assigned somewhere. A valid partition could be growth above 10%, growth from 0% through 10%, and negative growth, with zero explicitly in the middle category. Assign justified probabilities to those revised events. There are 100 cases, of which 80 are flagged, and 900 noncases, of which 90 are flagged. Of 170 flagged observations, 80 are cases: 80/170 ≈ 47.06%. Detection rate and the probability conditional on a flag are different. Without insurance, the expected net outcome is −$5. With full insurance and a certain premium, it is −$6 in either state. A risk-neutral expected-money objective favors being uninsured by $1. Loss aversion, risk aversion or a cash constraint can create a preference for insurance. Actual policies can also have exclusions and counterparty risk absent here. Expected project payoff is 0.6 × 80 − 0.4 × 40 = $32, exceeding zero. With perfect information, undertake it only in the favorable state: 0.6 × 80 + 0.4 × 0 = $48. Gross information value is $16 under the objective and assumptions. The bad-state obligation exceeds available cash by $20. Unless the contract or another feasible arrangement avoids or finances it, the project violates the stated constraint. Averaging across states does not produce cash in the bad state. The omitted strategies create selection and survivorship concerns. Ask for the full defined population, launch/closure dates, returns, costs, benchmarks and closure reasons. Reconstruct evaluation using the information available at each date. The critique should identify the threat without inventing the missing performance. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why a high detection rate does not imply a high probability of the condition after a positive signal. Use counts to make the denominator visible. Delayed check: reconstruct a two-state decision, vary the success probability, and identify the threshold at which the recommendation changes. Then add a cash constraint and explain its separate role. Where this leads Probability formalizes distributions and conditioning. Statistics quantifies sampling uncertainty. Econometrics evaluates identification. Finance analyzes risk-bearing, hedging and asset prices. Decision theory connects evidence to actions through objectives and losses. The foundational habit is to keep outcomes, beliefs, evidence and constraints distinct."}, {"title": "01.09 · Money, credit and financial institutions", "url": "v01-c09.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Trace the claims before telling the story Money and credit become easier to understand when you identify who holds an asset and who owes the corresponding obligation. A household's deposit is its claim on a bank. A bank's loan is its claim on a borrower. These relationships do not become clearer when described only as money “moving around.” Write the parties' balance-sheet changes. Money commonly serves as a means of payment, a unit in which prices and obligations are stated, and a way to carry purchasing power across time. These functions need not be perfectly fulfilled by every instrument. Inflation changes purchasing power; a payment arrangement can impose access or settlement constraints; an asset may be valuable without being routinely accepted for payment. A loan is a credit contract. It specifies an advance or borrowing right, repayment obligations, timing, interest and other terms. The borrowing is an asset acquisition and obligation at origination, not earned income. The lender's expected economic value depends on repayment, default, costs and discounting, not only the face amount promised. Deposits and reserves are different claims In a simplified two-tier banking model, customers hold commercial-bank deposits, while banks hold reserve balances used to settle with one another through the central bank's accounts. A customer's balance and the bank's reserve balance are different instruments held by different entities. A deposit liability is not automatically matched by an equal quantity of cash held aside for that specific depositor. A bank holds a portfolio of assets and finances it with deposits, other liabilities and equity. Its ability to meet payments depends on available liquidity and funding, while its solvency depends on the value of its assets relative to obligations. The Bank of England's 2014 account explains that commercial-bank lending can create deposits and that a simple story of banks mechanically multiplying a fixed quantity of central-bank money is misleading. The discussion is a conceptual source, not an assertion about every jurisdiction's current operating rules. Bank of England, Money creation in the modern economy . A loan can create a deposit Consider a modeled bank granting a loan and crediting the borrower's deposit at that same bank. The bank records a loan asset and a matching deposit liability. The borrower records a deposit asset and a loan liability. Neither party gains equity merely from recording equal new claims at the same initial amount. This accounting possibility does not imply unlimited profitable lending. The bank must assess repayment risk, expected income and costs, capital, liquidity, funding, contractual obligations and applicable rules. Borrowers must want and qualify for credit. Subsequent payments can require settlement resources. An accounting entry is not the complete economics of a sustainable banking business. Paying loan principal using a deposit at the same bank can reduce both the loan asset and the deposit liability. Interest has a different effect because it is a payment for financing under the contract and can affect income and equity. Record the allocation between principal, interest and fees explicitly. Worked example 1 — Origination and a payment to another bank Bank A begins with reserves of $100 and loans of $400. It owes customer deposits of $450 and has equity of $50. Its identity is 100 + 400 = 450 + 50. It grants a new $50 loan and credits the borrower's deposit. Loans rise to $450 and deposits to $500. Reserves remain $100 and equity $50. Assets and liabilities plus equity both become $550. The borrower pays $40 to a seller at Bank B. Assume settlement transfers $40 of reserves from A to B. Bank A's reserves fall to $60 and deposits to $460. Its loans remain $450. Its balance sheet is 60 + 450 = 460 + 50. Bank B gains $40 reserves and records a $40 deposit for the seller. Its equity does not change from that payment alone. The cross-bank payment changes the location of deposits and reserves. It does not mean that the borrower's loan vanished. If the borrower later repays $10 of principal using a deposit at A, A's loans and deposits both fall $10: 60 + 440 = 450 + 50. Every step identifies the entity and the paired entries. The example assumes available settlement resources and no fees or default. Different settlement arrangements require corresponding additional entries; the accounting must match the specified institution. Payments, settlement and liquidity A payment instruction, the customer's balance change, and final settlement are conceptually distinct steps. An instruction might be authorized before the receiving party has an unconditional settled claim. A payment arrangement defines how obligations are transferred or discharged and what happens if a participant cannot meet them. Liquidity is the ability to obtain the resources needed to meet obligations when due at acceptable cost. An institution can own valuable assets and still have difficulty obtaining cash quickly. Selling a long-horizon asset immediately may require a discount; borrowing can require collateral and willing counterparties. Solvency concerns whether assets adequately cover obligations under an appropriate economic valuation. A shortfall of immediate cash is not identical to negative net worth. They can interact: distress sales can reduce asset values, and doubts about solvency can make funding harder to obtain. Later banking and crisis chapters analyze the feedback. Worked example 2 — A withdrawal and a credit loss Start again with a bank holding $100 of immediately usable settlement resources and $400 of loans, financed by $450 deposits and $50 equity. Assume no other claims or valuation differences. Depositors withdraw $80 using those resources. Cash/reserves fall to $20 and deposits to $370. Assets are now $420 and liabilities $370. Equity remains $50: withdrawals reduce an asset and a liability together. A further immediate $30 withdrawal exceeds the $20 available resources. The bank needs another feasible funding or asset-conversion arrangement. Separately, suppose the loans lose $60 of economic value and the loss is recognized. Loan value becomes $340; total assets become $360. With liabilities still $370, economic equity becomes −$10. The cash shortfall and value shortfall are different problems. Borrowing another $10 can cover a payment only if financing is available; it adds both cash and an obligation and does not automatically repair negative equity. New equity, asset recovery or a restructuring can affect solvency in different ways. A real response requires contractual, institutional and legal analysis. Other financial institutions and securities A bond represents a specified debt claim. Equity represents an ownership claim whose payoff depends on the entity and the rights attached to the instrument. Funds pool investments under a mandate. Insurers combine underwriting, investment and contractual payout obligations. Exchanges and intermediaries support trading and transfer of claims. The labels do not remove risk. A debt promise can default. An ownership claim can lose value. An investment vehicle can have redemption constraints and fees. To understand an instrument, identify cash flows, claim priority, contingencies, timing, liquidity and governance. Later volumes develop valuation and specific structures. Intermediation can provide useful services: collecting information, monitoring borrowers, matching financial needs, pooling risks and facilitating payments. It can also create incentive conflicts and fragility. Evaluate the actual mechanism rather than assuming that every intermediary performs the same function. Central-bank roles and limits of a simple story A central bank can occupy roles involving currency, settlement, monetary policy and financial stability, with responsibilities depending on its legal and institutional setting. Commercial banks and a central bank have different balance sheets, objectives and powers. A central-bank asset purchase exchanges claims; understanding its effects requires tracing the counterparties and payment mechanism. Do not infer that expanding one monetary quantity guarantees a fixed increase in lending, prices or output. The consequences depend on behavior, constraints, expectations and the policy framework. Monetary economics later connects these mechanisms to equilibrium and evidence. For any current institutional claim, identify the jurisdiction and date and use the relevant official source. This introductory model does not state current reserve requirements, deposit-insurance limits, policy rates or emergency lending terms. Those are dated facts, while the paired-entry reasoning is a reusable analytical tool. Exercises A bank has $80 reserves, $320 loans, $350 deposits and $50 equity. It grants a $40 loan by crediting a deposit. Write its new balance sheet and the borrower's paired entries. What happens to equity at origination? Following Exercise 1, the borrower pays $25 to a customer at another bank, settled by transferring reserves. Show the first bank's new reserves, loans, deposits and equity. Show the receiving bank's transaction changes. Following Exercise 2, $15 of loan principal is repaid from a deposit at the first bank. Show the balance-sheet changes and closing identity, excluding interest and fees. A bank has assets of $600, liabilities of $540 and equity of $60. It recognizes a $75 asset-value loss. Find closing assets and equity with liabilities unchanged. Explain why borrowing $20 at face value does not fix the net-worth shortfall by itself. An institution has valuable assets of $200 due next year, $10 cash, and a $25 payment due today. Assume it has positive net worth. Identify the immediate liquidity problem and two possible responses whose feasibility would need checking. A household borrows $500 and immediately buys equipment for $500. Ignoring fees and valuation differences, trace its cash, equipment, debt and net-worth changes. Explain why the borrowing is not $500 of income. Someone says, “If banks can create deposits when lending, they can lend profitably without limits.” Identify at least five omitted economic or institutional constraints. Compare a deposit, a bond and an equity claim using holder, issuer obligation, contingent payoff, timing and liquidity. Explain why neither the name nor the promised face value proves safety. Solutions and evaluation criteria The bank has reserves 80, loans 360, deposits 390 and equity 50: 440 = 390 + 50. The borrower gains a $40 deposit asset and a $40 loan obligation. Equity is unchanged at initial equal values. The first bank has reserves 55, loans 360, deposits 365 and equity 50: 415 = 365 + 50. The receiving bank gains $25 reserves and a matching $25 customer deposit liability, with no equity change from the transfer alone. Loans become 345 and deposits 350; reserves remain 55 and equity 50. Assets are 400 = 350 + 50. Principal repayment reduces both loan asset and deposit liability. Assets fall to 525, so equity is 525 − 540 = −$15. Borrowing $20 raises both assets and liabilities by $20, leaving equity −$15 absent other consequences. The institution lacks $15 of immediately usable resources. It might sell or pledge an asset, obtain a loan, secure a capital contribution or renegotiate timing. Each option has costs and conditions; positive net worth does not guarantee immediate access. Loan origination raises cash and debt by $500. The purchase exchanges the $500 cash for $500 equipment. Net worth remains unchanged in the specified initial-value accounting. Income arises from earning or relevant subsequent gains, not the equal borrowing entries. Relevant constraints include borrower demand, creditworthiness, expected losses, funding costs, capital, liquidity and settlement, regulation, collateral, competition and risk appetite. The answer should explain how several affect feasible or profitable lending. Identify the actual contract and parties. A deposit is a claim on a bank, a bond has specified debt terms, and equity has ownership rights and contingent residual outcomes. Liquidity and repayment depend on the instrument and setting. Do not invent universal insurance or legal guarantees. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: trace a loan, an interbank payment, and a principal repayment on both sides of the transactions. Explain the distinction between reserve resources and a customer's deposit. Delayed check: construct a balance-sheet case with a payment shortage but positive net worth, then one with enough immediate cash but negative economic net worth. Explain why the remedies differ. Where this leads Accounting formalizes measurement. Corporate finance studies financing claims. Banking develops intermediation, capital and liquidity. Monetary economics examines policy transmission. Crisis analysis studies how claims, funding and asset values interact under stress. All depend on tracing who owes what to whom."}, {"title": "01.10 · Models, causation and economic arguments", "url": "v01-c10.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Four kinds of statement An economic argument can contain definitions, accounting identities, behavioral models, and empirical claims. Confusing them allows weak reasoning to appear stronger than it is. Ask what kind of statement is being made and what would establish it. A definition specifies how a concept is used. An identity follows from the defined components, such as assets equaling liabilities plus equity. A behavioral model describes how choices or outcomes depend on assumptions. An empirical claim concerns the world and requires evidence from an appropriate measurement and research design. A policy recommendation adds values and a choice among alternatives. Evidence that a policy raises total output does not alone establish that it should be adopted: distribution, costs, rights, feasibility and other objectives may matter. Separate the positive claim about consequences from the normative judgment about which consequences are desirable. Models isolate mechanisms A model deliberately abstracts from some features to examine others. It should define the actors, objectives, constraints, information, sequence of events and consistency conditions. Its conclusion follows conditionally from those choices. The question is whether the abstraction clarifies the mechanism relevant to the problem. “Holding other things fixed” makes a conditional comparison. A demand response to price with income fixed differs from a historical change in which price, income, product quality and expectations all moved. A model can help interpret that historical change, but the data do not automatically isolate the model's particular response. Assess a model by its logic, its relevance and its confrontation with evidence. An unrealistic assumption can still help isolate a useful mechanism, but a conclusion that depends critically on a false institutional feature can mislead. State which assumptions are essential and test how the conclusion changes when they are relaxed. Causation requires a counterfactual A causal effect compares what happens under an intervention with what would happen without that intervention for the relevant units and setting. The unobserved alternative is the counterfactual . Observing an outcome after an action does not reveal the counterfactual by itself. If a company trains workers and productivity later rises, possible explanations include training, improving demand, new equipment, worker selection or measurement changes. A causal claim needs a way to distinguish those mechanisms. Naming plausible alternatives is a useful start, but not an estimate of their magnitudes. Identification concerns whether the target causal or structural quantity can be recovered under the stated information and assumptions. Estimation then calculates it from finite data. A precisely estimated association can remain causally unidentified. Large sample size addresses some sampling uncertainty; it does not automatically repair confounding. Worked example 1 — Why a before-and-after change is incomplete A shop's weekly sales rise from $1,000 to $1,300 after it begins advertising. The manager attributes the entire $300 increase to the advertisement. During the same period, a comparable untreated shop's sales rise from $1,000 to $1,200. The treated shop's before-after increase is $300. The comparison shop's increase is $200. The difference in those changes is $100. Under an assumption that the comparison's $200 trend describes the treated shop's counterfactual change without advertising, the implied advertising effect is $100. The fourth step is the crucial assumption. The shops may face different customers, demand shocks or spillovers. They may not be comparable even if their original sales levels match. The $100 calculation is transparent, but its causal interpretation remains conditional on the design. This introduces the intuition of a difference-in-differences comparison. The advanced econometrics volume examines its assumptions, treatment timing, heterogeneous effects and inference in detail. A single illustrative pair of shops cannot validate those conditions. Randomization and comparisons Random assignment can make treatment and control groups comparable in expectation before the intervention, helping distinguish its effect from systematic selection. The intervention must be implemented and measured consistently. Attrition, noncompliance, interference across participants and chance imbalance can still complicate interpretation. Random assignment is different from random sampling. Assignment concerns who receives an intervention and can support internal causal comparisons. Sampling concerns who enters a study and influences how results generalize to a larger population. A randomized intervention among volunteers does not automatically identify effects for everyone elsewhere. Observational studies can also support causal reasoning when their design and assumptions are credible. Potential strategies include using a documented assignment threshold, an external source of variation or a carefully justified comparison. Each has conditions and limitations. The label attached to a method cannot substitute for examining them. Worked example 2 — Describe an experiment's result honestly In an illustrative randomized study, 200 participants are assigned to a program and 200 to control. At the specified follow-up, 40 program participants and 60 control participants have the adverse outcome. Assume outcomes are observed for everyone, assignment is implemented as intended and there are no spillovers. The observed program rate is 40/200 = 20%. The observed control rate is 60/200 = 30%. The observed difference is −10 percentage points. The relative reduction compared with the control rate is (30% − 20%)/30% = 1/3, approximately 33.33%. State the population, intervention, outcome and horizon. These figures are sample results, not proof that the exact population effect is ten percentage points or that the same effect applies in a different setting. Statistical inference quantifies sampling or assignment uncertainty under appropriate assumptions. For a policy decision, also ask about costs, implementation, other outcomes and distribution. A causally interpretable benefit is important evidence, but it is not the entire decision criterion. Prediction answers a different question A predictor aims to forecast an outcome using information available before the forecast target is realized. A causal analysis asks what would happen if an action were changed. A variable can be highly predictive without being a useful intervention. An umbrella can predict rain because people carry one when they expect rain. Giving everyone an umbrella does not create rainfall. In an economic setting, a company's expansion announcement may predict future sales because management already knows demand is improving. Changing the announcement alone need not generate the sales increase. Predictive evaluation must respect timing. If a backtest uses next month's information in a supposed decision today, it benefits from leakage. If a model is repeatedly modified until it fits the test period, that period is no longer an independent test. Later computation and econometrics provide disciplined evaluation procedures. Falsifiability and competing explanations A useful claim states what observations would be consistent with it and what would count against it. “The policy always works, except whenever it does not” provides no meaningful test. A model can specify conditional predictions whose validity depends on measurable conditions. Do not treat failure to reject a claim as proof that it is true. Evidence can be insufficient, measurement weak, or the study underpowered. Likewise, a statistically detectable association can be economically small or irrelevant to the decision. Distinguish correctness, precision, magnitude and practical consequence. Compare explanations through different predicted observations or designs that separate them. If both a supply shock and a demand shock could explain a price increase, examine the quantity change, input costs, institutional details and credible sources of variation. Several mechanisms can operate together; a forced single-cause story can be wrong. Make the argument auditable A strong argument names the question, defines the target quantity, states the model or design, presents the evidence and explains uncertainty. It separates observed facts from inference and gives the alternatives that remain plausible. A reader should be able to follow why each step supports the next. Sources should support the associated claim, not merely discuss the same topic. Dates and units matter. An opinion article, a dataset release, a theoretical paper and a causal study have different roles. Use each for what it actually establishes. Economic reasoning remains useful even before advanced methods are learned. The essential discipline is to avoid moving from identity to behavior, from correlation to causation, from prediction to intervention, or from one valued outcome to an entire policy conclusion without explaining the additional assumptions. Exercises Classify these statements as definition, identity, behavioral hypothesis, empirical claim or recommendation: “Equity equals assets minus liabilities”; “Consumers buy less when price rises, other relevant influences fixed”; “The observed program group earned 12% more”; “The government should adopt the program”; “A stock is measured at a point in time.” A firm reports sales rising from $500 to $650 after a pricing change. An untreated comparison rises from $400 to $500. Calculate both changes and their difference. State the assumption required for the difference to represent the pricing change's causal effect. In a randomized illustrative study, 100 of 500 treated participants and 150 of 500 controls experience an adverse outcome. Calculate both rates, their percentage-point difference and the relative reduction. Explain why uncertainty still matters. A survey finds that people with more schooling have higher income. Identify three possible explanations for the association and explain what a causal effect of additional schooling would compare. Explain the difference between random sampling and random assignment. Give a setting with assignment but a limited sampled population. A prediction model for defaults uses a field recorded only after a borrower defaults. Explain the evaluation error and how an honest forecasting dataset should be constructed. A researcher finds no statistically significant effect and concludes that the intervention has no effect. Identify missing information and give a more careful interpretation. Write a causal claim about an economic choice. State its counterfactual, one confounder, a possible design, an assumption that design needs, and one limit to generalizing the result. Solutions and evaluation criteria Respectively: identity; behavioral hypothesis; empirical descriptive claim; normative recommendation; definition. The reported group difference alone is not necessarily causal. The treated change is $150 and comparison change $100, leaving $50. Causal interpretation requires the comparison's change to represent the treated firm's counterfactual trend without its pricing change, with relevant spillovers or differential shocks addressed. The rates are 20% and 30%, a −10 percentage-point treated-minus-control difference and approximately 33.33% relative reduction. These are realized sample rates; inference is needed for uncertainty about the target effect, and external validity is separate. Schooling may increase skills; family resources may affect both schooling and income; ability, location or selection may contribute. A causal effect compares the same relevant person's or population's income under differing schooling interventions, with other design conditions specified. Sampling determines study inclusion; assignment determines treatment among included units. Randomized assignment within a volunteer group can support an internal comparison without establishing that the group represents all possible recipients. The model uses post-outcome information unavailable at forecast time, causing leakage. Freeze features according to their actual availability at each decision date, preserve a genuinely held-out evaluation, and document timing. Needed information includes the effect estimate, interval, study size, power, design, measurement and relevant effect magnitude. A nonsignificant result can be consistent with small, zero or sizable effects depending on the uncertainty. State what the evidence can and cannot exclude. A strong answer defines an intervention and outcome, identifies the counterfactual, explains the confounding mechanism, gives a feasible design and its identifying assumptions, and recognizes a population or institutional limit. Several well-supported designs are possible. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain how an identity can be true without identifying the response to an intervention. Then explain why a highly predictive variable might be a poor policy lever. Delayed check: find an economic causal claim in a public discussion. Reconstruct its counterfactual, evidence, assumptions and feasible competing explanation without appealing to the speaker's status. Where this leads Economic theory clarifies mechanisms and consistency. Econometrics formalizes identification and inference. Research practice evaluates contribution and reproducibility. Teaching and leadership require communicating those distinctions clearly when decisions must be made despite incomplete evidence."}, {"title": "01.11 · Reading, writing and explaining analysis", "url": "v01-c11.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Make reasoning visible to another person Analysis has limited value if another person cannot determine what was asked, what was measured, how the conclusion follows, and what could change it. Good communication is part of technical competence. It forces the analyst to identify hidden assumptions and separate a defensible inference from an attractive story. Begin with the question. “Describe unemployment” is broad; “Did the unemployment rate fall because employment rose or because participation changed?” directs attention to particular counts and mechanisms. “Should we expand?” requires a comparison among feasible actions, not just a forecast of revenue. Write the conclusion early, with the condition that matters most. Then present the evidence and reasoning needed to assess it. A reader should not have to search through computations to discover whether the project was recommended, which alternative was considered, or which assumption makes the difference. A reusable analytical memo A concise memo can follow five elements: the question and decision; the recommendation or finding; the mechanism and calculation; uncertainty and alternatives; and the action or evidence needed next. The arrangement can change with the audience, but the chain of reasoning must remain clear. For an executive, a central risk and its consequence can matter more than the derivation's intermediate lines. For a technical reviewer, the derivation and identification assumptions must be accessible. Keep supporting detail in a table or appendix rather than replacing it with unexplained authority. State who benefits, who bears costs and which objective guides the recommendation. “This is optimal” is incomplete without specifying the objective and constraints. An economically positive project may conflict with another institutional priority, face financing constraints, or distribute costs unequally. Distinguish facts, assumptions and inference A measured fact might be the observed sale count in a supplied dataset. An assumption might be that its reporting coverage is consistent through time. An inference might be that demand changed. A recommendation might be to increase capacity. Label the transitions rather than presenting all four as equally established facts. Use language that matches the evidence. “The supplied records show,” “under the stated assumption,” “this is consistent with,” and “the design identifies” have different meanings. Avoid changing “associated with” to “caused by” during a summary. Concision should preserve the epistemic status of the claim. Quantify an assumption's relevance when possible. “Results are sensitive” is vague. “The recommendation switches when the success probability falls below 60%” identifies a decision threshold. “The effect's plausible range includes outcomes that would reverse the decision” explains why uncertainty matters. Worked example 1 — Rewrite a revenue-only recommendation An analyst writes: “Buy the equipment. It will bring in $600 next year and $600 the year after. The total is $1,200, so we make $200.” The equipment costs $1,000 now, and the specified opportunity rate is 10% annually. A more complete version is: Under the supplied certain net-cash-flow assumptions, purchase is better than the specified 10% opportunity by $41.32 in present value: −1,000 + 600/1.1 + 600/1.1². The recommendation requires the forecast to include all relevant operating costs and the purchase to be financeable. At a 20% discount rate, NPV is −$83.33. Verify the cash-flow forecast and rate before committing. The revised memo names the objective, timing, alternative and assumptions. It does not convert unverified forecasts into facts. It also distinguishes net receipts from revenue, a difference that could materially change the calculation. Tables and graphs are arguments A table should make comparisons easy to inspect. Put like quantities in the same column, give units in headers, identify the time period, and include denominators when rates matter. Totals and subtotals should reconcile. A note should explain missing values, rounding and relevant measurement choices. A graph must label axes and scales and identify the data or model it represents. A bar chart with a truncated axis can exaggerate differences; a time-series plot can use a narrower range appropriately if its purpose and scale are explicit. The issue is whether the presentation makes the comparison understandable rather than manipulating its impression. A plotted line connecting observations can suggest continuous measurement or a trend model. A diagram of a theoretical relationship is different from a graph of observed data. Label it as a model or illustrative example when that is what it is. Do not add fabricated empirical observations for visual polish. Worked example 2 — Show counts as well as rates A report says, “Unemployment improved from 10% to 9%.” The underlying illustrative labor-force counts are: Period Labor force Unemployed Unemployment rate 0 1,000 people 100 people 10% 1 1,200 people 108 people 9% A careful summary is: “The unemployment rate fell one percentage point, while the unemployed count rose 8%. The labor force increased 20%, changing the denominator. These figures alone do not establish the cause or the change in every person's labor-market experience.” The table preserves the facts needed to assess the claim. Its purpose is not to declare whether every aspect improved or worsened; it separates the measured quantities before evaluating them. Source provenance and reproducibility A citation should let a reader locate the material supporting a claim. Record the source, dataset or series identifier, date or version, measurement units, and transformations. For a hypothetical example, say that its numbers are constructed. A reader should never have to guess whether a figure is observed or invented for teaching. Reproducibility means another person can follow the documented inputs and procedure to obtain the stated output, within explained numerical differences. For an early manual calculation, supply the original values and steps. For later computational work, provide the code, data access instructions, environment and relevant settings. Reproducing a result is important but does not prove that its interpretation is correct. A biased analysis can reproduce perfectly. Keep computational reliability, measurement validity and causal interpretation as distinct review questions. Explain a mechanism before its vocabulary Technical vocabulary is efficient once a concept is understood. Introduce the idea first: an extra book requires giving up some meals under a budget. Then name the opportunity cost and slope. If the learner can recite the term but cannot reason through a new example, the instruction has not achieved transfer. Use multiple representations carefully. An intuitive story, a balance-sheet table, an equation and a graph can describe the same mechanism. Check that they agree. An analogy helps when its relevant mapping is explicit; it misleads when an irrelevant feature of the analogy becomes part of the argument. Ask learners to reconstruct a result rather than merely recognize it. “Explain why principal repayment leaves initial-value net worth unchanged” is more diagnostic than “Is principal repayment an expense?” Follow with a case containing interest or fees to see whether the distinction survives a changed problem. Read actively and fairly When reading an argument, reconstruct its strongest defensible version before criticizing it. Identify its question, definitions, assumptions, evidence and conclusion. Determine whether the objection concerns mathematical correctness, measurement, identification, generalization or the objective being optimized. A disagreement about values should not be disguised as a false arithmetic claim. A disagreement about causal identification should not be dismissed as a difference in taste. Precise criticism helps the author revise the part that actually needs work. Keep a record of questions rather than copying every sentence into notes. For each important result, write its mechanism, conditions and one counterexample or boundary case. Return later and derive it without looking. This practice creates a testable record of understanding. Editing for clarity Remove repetition, unsupported adjectives and claims of importance that do not explain a consequence. Replace “it is obvious” with the reasoning. Replace an unexplained acronym with a definition where it first appears. Use units and concrete verbs. A sentence should contribute a fact, a logical step, a condition or a decision-relevant implication. Keep essential qualifications near the claim they limit. Hiding all assumptions at the end of a long document can allow the main text to overstate its conclusion. The aim is readable rigor: enough detail to examine the reasoning, organized so the reader can find it. Exercises Rewrite: “Profit grew 200%, proving the strategy worked.” Specify the missing base, period, population, costs and causal evidence. Give a version that reports only what the supplied statement could establish. A project costs $500 now and pays a certain $600 in one year. At a 10% opportunity rate, calculate NPV and write a 75-word memo including timing, assumptions and the financing question. Build a table for two divisions: A has $200 revenue and $50 profit; B has $800 revenue and $40 profit. Include revenues, profits and margins and calculate the consolidated margin. Write one sentence explaining why an equal-weight average answers a different question. Rewrite: “Inflation fell from 6% to 2%, so prices became cheaper.” State the correct inference and give an illustrative two-period index path starting at 100. Read an economic claim and classify each supporting item as measured evidence, assumption, calculation, inference or recommendation. Identify a transition that would need additional justification. Design a provenance note for a hypothetical chart of annual real wages. Specify the information a reader needs, including how “real” is defined. Explain what changes if the numbers are constructed for teaching. Prepare a two-minute explanation of why a loan can increase cash without increasing equity at origination. Use one table and one everyday explanation, then ask a question that tests transfer. Give a fair critique of this claim: “Our randomized study among 100 volunteers identifies the effect of the program everywhere.” Separate the possible internal validity from external validity and list further evidence needed. Solutions and evaluation criteria A 200% increase requires a defined positive base and comparable period. Even if measured correctly, it is a descriptive result, not proof of causation. A careful rewrite states the specific observed profit change, its accounting basis and period, then says the strategy's causal contribution remains to be established. NPV is −500 + 600/1.1 = $45.4545… approximately $45.45. A strong memo gives a conditional recommendation, identifies the assumed complete certain net payment, names the 10% alternative and checks whether financing is feasible. Its word count should not force omission of the decisive assumptions. A's margin is 25%; B's is 5%. Total profit is $90 on $1,000 revenue, so the consolidated margin is 9%. Equal weighting gives 15% for a typical two-division average, while consolidated performance requires revenue weights. The positive inflation rate slowed. An index rises from 100 to 106, then to 108.12. Prices in that index continue rising in the second period; they do not fall. Assess whether categories are separated accurately and whether the chosen transition needs an additional measurement, identifying assumption or normative criterion. The content of the selected claim determines the answer. Record the wage source/series, population, hours basis, period, currency, nominal measure, price index, base year, vintage and transformation. If constructed, label the chart illustrative and supply the invented values without presenting them as observations. The table should add equal cash and debt at initial equal values, leaving equity unchanged. A good transfer question adds a fee, interest or asset purchase and asks which entries differ. Evaluate the learner's reconstruction, not their recognition of a slogan. Random assignment can support a causal comparison among participants under implementation, measurement and interference assumptions. Generalization to other populations and institutions needs evidence about representativeness, mechanisms, treatment differences and context. The sample size also affects precision; it does not alone invalidate randomization. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: present a finding in 30 seconds, then defend it for five minutes. The short version must preserve the principal assumption and uncertainty; the long version must expose the reasoning. Delayed check: revisit an old memo and reconstruct its calculations from the documented inputs alone. Identify one claim whose wording is stronger than its evidence, if any, and revise it. Where this leads Research papers require an explicit contribution and auditable methods. Teaching requires diagnosing understanding. Institutional leadership requires decisions and accountability. Clear communication is the bridge between analysis and those responsibilities."}, {"title": "01.12 · Foundation integration and diagnostic", "url": "v01-c12.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Integrate the foundations before moving ahead The earlier chapters taught separate habits: compare feasible alternatives, preserve units and denominators, express relationships, reconcile stocks and flows, discount dated cash flows, trace incentives, deflate money amounts, reason under uncertainty, identify financial claims, evaluate causal arguments and communicate clearly. This chapter puts those habits into one case. Integration is a test of whether you can decide which tool applies. A formula named in a question is easier to recognize than a method hidden inside a realistic narrative. Begin by organizing the case into entities, dates, units, decisions and assumptions. Then perform calculations in a sequence that allows independent checks. Every number below is constructed for instruction. The case is deliberately simplified: it excludes taxes, depreciation and many contract details so the relevant relationships can be seen. Those exclusions must remain visible. Later volumes add the omitted economics and accounting rather than pretending the simplified case is a complete business model. The business case An owner contributes $1,800 to a newly formed business. The business borrows $1,200, purchases equipment for $1,000 cash and inventory for $800 cash, and sells the entire inventory for $1,200 cash. It pays $60 interest during the illustrative period. No principal is repaid. There are no other transactions, expenses or valuation changes. The questions are: What happened to cash and equity? Did the business earn its borrowing as revenue? Can its future investment proposal be judged from revenue alone? Which conclusions follow by accounting, and which need behavioral or empirical evidence? Worked example 1 — Statements that reconcile Cash changes are +1,800 +1,200 −1,000 −800 +1,200 −60 = +$2,340 from a zero opening balance. Revenue is $1,200. Inventory expense is $800, and interest expense is $60. Profit is $340. Closing assets are cash $2,340 plus equipment $1,000 = $3,340. Closing liabilities are $1,200. Equity is the $1,800 contribution plus $340 retained profit = $2,140. The identity holds: 3,340 = 1,200 + 2,140. The cash inflow from borrowing is financing, not sales revenue. The equipment purchase converts cash into another asset. This simplified case explicitly omits depreciation; introducing it would affect equipment's carrying amount and profit consistently, while not creating an additional cash payment at recognition. For cash-flow presentation, classification can depend on the applicable rules. An internal analysis can separately show operating receipts and inventory payments of $400 net, an interest payment of $60, equipment investment of −$1,000, and initial financing of $3,000. Their sum remains $2,340 regardless of how the stated interest payment is grouped. A future investment proposal For the next one-year period, the business can spend $800 immediately on a separate project. Its end-of-year net receipt is $1,200 in a favorable state and $700 in an unfavorable state. The assigned favorable-state probability is 0.6. Assume these receipts already include all subsequent project costs; the initial $800 is not included in them. The expected receipt is 0.6 × 1,200 + 0.4 × 700 = $1,000. At a specified 10% discount benchmark, the discounted expectation minus the initial expenditure is 1,000/1.1 − 800 ≈ $109.09. This is a transparent benchmark calculation, not a proof that 10% properly prices the project's risk. At the same benchmark, the favorable-state NPV is 1,200/1.1 − 800 ≈ $290.91. The unfavorable-state NPV is 700/1.1 − 800 ≈ −$163.64. The expected calculation does not remove the 40% unfavorable-state probability or tell us whether the owner can absorb it. Let p be the favorable-state probability. The expected receipt is 700 + 500p. The benchmark NPV is positive when (700 + 500p)/1.1 > 800, or p > 0.36. A probability estimate near that threshold is decision-relevant; an unexamined point estimate is insufficient evidence. The household financing case A different prospective owner has only $500 of cash available for the same $800 project. A loan of $300 is offered with a $330 end-of-year repayment. Keeping and investing the $500 instead produces $550 in one year under a specified certain 10% opportunity. The project receipts are as above, and the new loan repayment must be deducted separately. Worked example 2 — Expected advantage with a cash constraint At initiation, $500 existing cash plus $300 loan proceeds funds the $800 cost. Remaining cash is zero. In the favorable state, final cash after repayment is 1,200 − 330 = $870. In the unfavorable state, final cash is 700 − 330 = $370. Expected final cash is 0.6 × 870 + 0.4 × 370 = $670. The alternative produces $550, so the project exceeds it by $120 in expected year-end cash. Relative to that alternative, the project gives +$320 in the favorable state and −$180 in the unfavorable state. The expected advantage is not a recommendation for every household. Starting with zero remaining cash can violate the person's needs for interim spending or buffers. If an additional $100 payment can arise before project receipts and no funding is available, the proposal may be infeasible even though its expectation is favorable. Do not subtract the original $500 again from only one alternative. Both use the same starting cash. Comparing year-end wealth under consistent actions already accounts for it. If expressing each action's NPV instead, apply the same valuation date and complete cash flows to both. Add purchasing power and evidence If the applicable price index rises 5% over the year, final cash amounts expressed in initial purchasing power are divided by 1.05. The project expectation becomes 670/1.05 ≈ $638.10 and the alternative 550/1.05 ≈ $523.81. This common deflation preserves their comparison but clarifies the units. The favorable-state probability needs support. Was it estimated from comparable projects? Were failed projects omitted? Did the economic environment change? Is the receipt forecast genuinely net of all future costs? These questions concern measurement and evidence, not the arithmetic of a supplied probability. Suppose the business's sales rose after buying the equipment. The rise is not by itself a causal estimate of the equipment's effect. Demand, staffing, prices and seasonality can also change. A comparison design needs an explicit counterfactual. Accounting can document sales; it cannot independently establish the causal contribution of the equipment. Use an integration checklist Entity: distinguish business, owner, borrower, bank and economy. Time: identify the valuation date and every payment date. Units: separate money, rates, percentages, percentage points and real price bases. Accounting: reconcile all stocks and flows. Decision: name the best feasible alternative and incremental consequences. Uncertainty: specify outcomes, probabilities and constraints. Mechanism: identify incentives and consistency conditions. Evidence: separate constructed inputs, measured facts and causal inference. Communication: state a conclusion with the assumptions that could change it. A mistake found during integration often points to a specific prerequisite. Incorrect rate bases lead back to Chapter 2. Unreconciled financing leads to Chapter 4 or 9. Discounting inconsistency leads to Chapter 5. Unsupported probabilities lead to Chapter 8. Causal overstatement leads to Chapter 10. Use this map for targeted remediation. Exercises Reconstruct the business's balance sheet and profit from the case without looking at Worked Example 1. Then suppose it pays another $100 of principal from cash. Show the new cash, assets, liabilities and equity. For the business case, add a $100 depreciation expense and a corresponding $100 reduction in equipment carrying value. Keep the actual cash transactions unchanged. Recompute profit, cash, assets and equity and reconcile the identity. Calculate the project's expected receipt and 10%-benchmark NPV when the favorable-state probability is 0.3 and when it is 0.5. Verify the probability threshold for positive benchmark NPV. For the household financing case, change the year-end loan repayment from $330 to $360. Recompute both final-cash outcomes, expected final cash and the expected advantage over the $550 alternative. In the original household financing case, vary the favorable-state probability p. Derive expected final cash and the threshold at which it exceeds the $550 alternative. With a 5% price-index increase, express the original project's favorable and unfavorable household final cash and the alternative in initial purchasing power. Explain why a common deflator does not settle a preference over risk. A promoter reports a 60% favorable-state rate based only on businesses still operating after five years. Write a critique identifying selection, comparability, timing and the data needed to assess the estimate. Write a 200-word investment memo using the household financing case. Include a conditional decision, figures that reconcile, the alternative, probability sensitivity, risk and interim liquidity, a price basis, and a fact that could change the decision. Present no guaranteed outcome. Solutions and evaluation criteria Before repayment, cash is 2,340, equipment 1,000, assets 3,340, debt 1,200 and equity 2,140; profit is 340. Principal repayment reduces cash to 2,240 and debt to 1,100. Assets become 3,240 and equity remains 2,140: 3,240 = 1,100 + 2,140. Profit becomes 240; cash remains 2,340 because this entry is not another cash payment. Equipment becomes 900, assets 3,240, liabilities 1,200 and equity 2,040: 3,240 = 1,200 + 2,040. This assumes the specified depreciation treatment and no tax effect. Expected receipt is 700 + 500p. At 0.3 it is 850, giving NPV 850/1.1 − 800 ≈ −$27.27. At 0.5 it is 950, giving approximately $63.64. Solving 700 + 500p = 880 gives p = 0.36. A risk-appropriate valuation still requires more than this benchmark. Final cash is 840 or 340. Expectation is 0.6 × 840 + 0.4 × 340 = 640. Expected advantage is 640 − 550 = $90, reduced by the additional $30 repayment. Expected final cash is 870p + 370(1 − p) = 370 + 500p. It exceeds 550 when p > 0.36. At the threshold it matches the alternative in expectation, while their outcome distributions remain different. Real amounts are 870/1.05 ≈ 828.57, 370/1.05 ≈ 352.38 and 550/1.05 ≈ 523.81. The common price basis does not remove uncertain states or establish the owner's utility, constraints or risk preference. Surviving businesses can be a selected subset, and the data may omit failures or differ from the proposed project. Seek a defined original cohort, all outcomes, start dates, industry and conditions, costs, reasons for exit and the information available at the forecast date. Do not assume the survivor rate equals the proposal's success probability. Evaluate consistency and conditional reasoning. The memo should distinguish expectation from a guarantee, give the relevant comparison, expose the liquidity constraint and data weakness, use a stated price basis and avoid double counting. A justified decision to proceed, reduce scale, seek more information or decline can be defensible under explicit additional assumptions. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the complete case to a beginner, then defend its accounting and uncertainty treatment to a technical reader. Both explanations must describe the same mechanism and assumptions. Delayed check: change the case's prices, loan terms and probabilities and reconstruct the analysis from the transaction list and timelines. Diagnose the first point of failure if your statements or comparisons do not reconcile. Readiness for the next volume You are ready for the mathematical toolkit when you can translate relationships into equations, preserve units, solve basic systems, compare dated amounts, distinguish probabilities from outcomes, and explain assumptions. Use the volume diagnostic and cumulative assessments to identify gaps. Advancing with an unresolved error in these foundations makes later sophisticated calculations less reliable. The next volume develops proof, calculus, linear algebra and optimization. Those tools will strengthen the same reasoning rather than replace it. The aim is to derive and evaluate unfamiliar results with confidence grounded in explicit steps."}, {"title": "02.01 · Mathematical language and proof", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Construct valid arguments using sets, functions, quantifiers and proof methods. Sets relations implication necessity and sufficiency induction contradiction counterexamples"}, {"title": "02.02 · Functions, limits and continuity", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Determine limiting behavior and distinguish continuity from differentiability. Domains composition sequences limits continuity intermediate-value reasoning"}, {"title": "02.03 · Derivatives and economic change", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive marginal quantities and elasticities using differentiation rules. Derivative definition product and chain rules implicit differentiation elasticity Taylor approximation"}, {"title": "02.04 · Integrals and accumulated quantities", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Recover totals from marginal quantities and evaluate definite integrals. Antiderivatives fundamental theorem substitution integration by parts improper integrals"}, {"title": "02.05 · Vectors, matrices and linear systems", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve linear systems and diagnose rank and identification. Matrix operations elimination rank inverse linear independence bases null space"}, {"title": "02.06 · Eigenvalues, projections and quadratic forms", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Interpret stability, least squares and covariance geometry using linear algebra. Eigenvectors positive definiteness orthogonal projection spectral decomposition singular values"}, {"title": "02.07 · Multivariable calculus", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Calculate gradients, Hessians and total derivatives in economic models. Partial derivatives Jacobian Hessian multivariate chain rule implicit-function theorem"}, {"title": "02.08 · Unconstrained and constrained optimization", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve constrained decisions and verify sufficient conditions rather than relying only on first-order conditions. Concavity Lagrange multipliers inequality constraints KKT corner solutions envelopes"}, {"title": "02.09 · Convexity, duality and comparative statics", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use curvature and duality to establish optimization results and parameter responses. Convex sets separating intuition value functions dual problems sensitivity comparative statics"}, {"title": "02.10 · Real analysis and convergence", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Prove convergence results and recognize when interchange of limits requires justification. Completeness compactness subsequences uniform convergence contraction mappings"}, {"title": "02.11 · Difference and differential equations", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve simple dynamic systems and classify local stability. First-order recursions systems steady states eigenvalue stability differential equations"}, {"title": "02.12 · Numerical methods and dynamic foundations", "url": "curriculum.html#v02-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement root finding and approximation with explicit accuracy criteria. Bisection Newton methods interpolation numerical integration conditioning Bellman equation introduction"}, {"title": "03.01 · Probability spaces and counting", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build coherent event probabilities and explain independence assumptions. Events axioms combinatorics independence inclusion-exclusion sampling with replacement"}, {"title": "03.02 · Conditional probability and Bayes", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Update beliefs correctly and diagnose base-rate errors. Conditional events Bayes rule total probability odds likelihood ratios"}, {"title": "03.03 · Random variables and distributions", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Work with discrete and continuous distributions and their support. PMF PDF CDF Bernoulli binomial Poisson normal exponential"}, {"title": "03.04 · Expectation, variance and covariance", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compute moments and explain diversification using joint distributions. Expected value variance covariance correlation transformations total expectation"}, {"title": "03.05 · Joint distributions and conditional expectation", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive conditional moments and identify dependence beyond correlation. Joint and marginal distributions conditioning total variance iterated expectations"}, {"title": "03.06 · Large samples and convergence", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Apply laws of large numbers and central-limit reasoning with stated conditions. Convergence modes LLN CLT heavy tails dependence finite-sample caveats"}, {"title": "03.07 · Sampling and estimation", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Construct estimators and compare bias, variance and consistency. Sampling designs point estimation standard errors maximum likelihood introduction bootstrap"}, {"title": "03.08 · Confidence intervals and hypothesis tests", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Interpret intervals and tests without confusing statistical and economic significance. Null and alternatives test statistics size power p-values multiple testing"}, {"title": "03.09 · Bayesian inference and decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Combine likelihoods, priors and losses to make transparent decisions. Posterior distributions conjugacy credible intervals predictive distributions sensitivity to priors"}, {"title": "03.10 · Expected utility and risk preferences", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve decisions under uncertainty and identify the limits of expected-utility models. Utility certainty equivalents risk premiums Jensen risk aversion ambiguity"}, {"title": "03.11 · Information, forecasting and scoring", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate predictions and the value of additional information using proper criteria. Calibration proper scoring conditional forecasts expected value of information loss functions"}, {"title": "03.12 · Measure and stochastic foundations", "url": "curriculum.html#v03-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain the formal probability tools needed for graduate econometrics and finance. Sigma-algebras measurability Lebesgue integration dominated convergence conditional expectation martingale introduction"}, {"title": "04.01 · Reproducible work and data provenance", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Create a workflow another analyst can reproduce from the supplied inputs. Files environments README version control data dictionaries vintages licenses"}, {"title": "04.02 · Spreadsheets and financial models", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build auditable spreadsheet calculations and detect hidden modeling errors. Cell references formulas cash-flow timelines reconciliation sensitivity tables audit checks"}, {"title": "04.03 · Python fundamentals for analysis", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write clear executable programs for economic calculations. Types loops functions exceptions assertions files standard library"}, {"title": "04.04 · Arrays, tables and SQL", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Transform structured data without corrupting units, keys or populations. Arrays joins aggregation SQL missing values dates duplicate keys"}, {"title": "04.05 · Visualization and descriptive evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Choose and interpret figures that expose rather than conceal evidence. Distributions time series scatterplots scales uncertainty misleading comparisons"}, {"title": "04.06 · Simulation and Monte Carlo", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate quantities by simulation and assess Monte Carlo error. Random seeds simulation design sampling uncertainty variance reduction reproducibility"}, {"title": "04.07 · Regression as projection", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive ordinary least squares and interpret fitted values and residuals. Projection normal equations coefficients residuals fit extrapolation"}, {"title": "04.08 · Identification and confounding", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Distinguish descriptive associations from identified causal effects. Potential outcomes introduction selection confounding controls bad controls identification"}, {"title": "04.09 · Prediction and model validation", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate models using splits that respect economic timing and information availability. Train-validation-test time splits leakage overfitting baseline comparisons losses"}, {"title": "04.10 · Time-series and panel data basics", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Prepare longitudinal data and identify dependence and aggregation pitfalls. Lags trends stationarity intuition panel keys survivorship frequencies deflation"}, {"title": "04.11 · Computational reliability and numerical audits", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Verify outputs through invariants, simple cases and independent calculations. Assertions tests edge cases units precision numerical conditioning peer reproduction"}, {"title": "04.12 · Empirical integration and replication", "url": "curriculum.html#v04-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reproduce a small empirical result and document departures from the original analysis. Source retrieval reconstruction robustness replication report limits of inference"}, {"title": "05.01 · Preferences and consumer choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive demands and identify corner solutions under a budget constraint. Preferences utility indifference curves budget sets Marshallian demand revealed preference"}, {"title": "05.02 · Demand, duality and welfare", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Decompose price responses and measure welfare with correct income concepts. Hicksian demand expenditure function Slutsky indirect utility compensating variation"}, {"title": "05.03 · Production, costs and firm choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive cost functions and output/input choices from technology. Production sets marginal products cost minimization fixed and sunk cost supply"}, {"title": "05.04 · Competitive equilibrium and surplus", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve partial equilibrium and distinguish efficiency from distribution. Market clearing consumer and producer surplus elasticity entry tax incidence"}, {"title": "05.05 · General equilibrium and welfare", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze exchange and production interactions across markets. Edgeworth box Walras law Pareto efficiency welfare theorems missing markets"}, {"title": "05.06 · Monopoly, oligopoly and pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve market-power models and compare price and quantity competition. Monopoly markup price discrimination Cournot Bertrand entry barriers"}, {"title": "05.07 · Game theory and strategic behavior", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify equilibria and credible strategies in static and dynamic games. Dominance Nash mixed strategies extensive form backward induction commitment"}, {"title": "05.08 · Information, signaling and selection", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain how asymmetric information changes trade and incentives. Adverse selection signaling screening pooling separating equilibria lemons"}, {"title": "05.09 · Contracts, incentives and organizations", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve simple principal-agent problems and evaluate incentive trade-offs. Moral hazard participation incentive compatibility monitoring multitasking firm boundaries"}, {"title": "05.10 · Externalities and public goods", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare corrective instruments under informational and institutional constraints. External costs Pigouvian taxes public goods free riding Coase conditions congestion"}, {"title": "05.11 · Auctions, matching and market design", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze allocation rules and incentive properties in designed markets. Auction formats revenue equivalence conditions matching stability strategy-proofness"}, {"title": "05.12 · Behavioral economics and microeconomic evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate departures from standard choice and the evidence needed to support interventions. Biases reference dependence time inconsistency experiments welfare interpretation replication"}, {"title": "06.01 · National accounts and macro measurement", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reconcile output, expenditure and income measures and explain what GDP omits. GDP value added expenditure identity deflators real income stocks versus flows"}, {"title": "06.02 · Growth and the Solow model", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive steady states and distinguish level effects from sustained growth. Capital accumulation depreciation population technology convergence growth accounting"}, {"title": "06.03 · Innovation, human capital and institutions", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare mechanisms of long-run development with appropriate evidence. Endogenous growth human capital diffusion institutions geography identification"}, {"title": "06.04 · Consumption, saving and investment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive intertemporal decisions and connect household and firm behavior to aggregates. Permanent income life cycle liquidity constraints investment adjustment costs expectations"}, {"title": "06.05 · Labor markets and unemployment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain employment dynamics using search, incentives and institutional mechanisms. Participation matching unemployment flows wage setting efficiency wages hysteresis"}, {"title": "06.06 · Money, banking and monetary transmission", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace monetary-policy mechanisms without relying on a mechanical deposit multiplier. Money demand policy rates reserves bank balance sheets credit transmission"}, {"title": "06.07 · Inflation, expectations and nominal rigidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain inflation dynamics and distinguish supply from demand disturbances. Quantity theory conditions Phillips curves expectations price stickiness shocks"}, {"title": "06.08 · Business cycles and stabilization models", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare short-run models and identify their assumptions and predictions. IS-LM aggregate demand-supply RBC introduction New Keynesian introduction stabilization"}, {"title": "06.09 · Fiscal policy and public debt", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive debt dynamics and evaluate fiscal trade-offs with explicit assumptions. Budget constraint debt-to-GDP multipliers automatic stabilizers fiscal space distribution"}, {"title": "06.10 · Trade and comparative advantage", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive gains and distributional effects from trade models. Ricardian factor endowments scale firms tariffs terms of trade"}, {"title": "06.11 · Exchange rates and external balances", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Connect international accounts, exchange-rate mechanisms and policy regimes. Balance of payments net foreign assets exchange rates PPP interest parity capital flows"}, {"title": "06.12 · Crises, policy regimes and macroeconomic judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare crisis mechanisms and evaluate a country case without claiming certainty from one model. Financial crises sudden stops sovereign distress historical comparison scenario analysis"}, {"title": "07.01 · Transactions and double-entry accounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Record transactions and preserve the accounting equation. Debits credits journals accruals recognition transaction analysis"}, {"title": "07.02 · Balance sheet, income statement and cash flow", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain and reconcile the three principal statements. Assets liabilities equity revenues expenses operating-investing-financing cash flows"}, {"title": "07.03 · Revenue, working capital and operating cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Model operating cash needs and distinguish reported sales from cash collection. Receivables inventory payables revenue timing cash conversion cycle allowances"}, {"title": "07.04 · Long-lived assets and investment accounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Track asset investment and distinguish accounting expense from economic depreciation. Capitalization depreciation amortization impairment disposals investment assets"}, {"title": "07.05 · Debt, leases and financing obligations", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze financing obligations and their effects on performance and cash flow. Borrowing interest leases covenants commitments effective interest disclosure"}, {"title": "07.06 · Equity, consolidation and ownership", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace equity claims and distinguish ownership, control and reported group results. Share capital retained earnings dilution minority interests consolidation concepts buybacks"}, {"title": "07.07 · Taxes, accruals and earnings quality", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify timing differences and assess sustainability of reported earnings. Current and deferred tax concepts accruals provisions one-offs manipulation incentives"}, {"title": "07.08 · Managerial accounting and cost decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use contribution margins and relevant costs for operational decisions. Cost behavior break-even allocation activity-based costing make-or-buy capacity"}, {"title": "07.09 · Financial ratios and business diagnosis", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Interpret ratios in context and explain accounting comparability limitations. Liquidity leverage margins turnover ROIC DuPont peer selection"}, {"title": "07.10 · Three-statement forecasting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build linked forecasts with a transparent financing policy. Revenue drivers operating expenses working capital capex financing cash balancing"}, {"title": "07.11 · Accounting standards, disclosure and comparability", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify where accounting-policy differences require adjustment and dated source verification. IFRS and US GAAP concepts disclosure estimates restatements segment reporting jurisdiction"}, {"title": "07.12 · Forensic analysis and accounting integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Diagnose a business using reconciled accounts and explicit alternative explanations. Cash-versus-profit gaps covenant risk red flags normalization business model economics"}, {"title": "08.01 · Value creation and capital budgeting", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate incremental project cash flows and reconcile NPV with decision criteria. NPV IRR opportunity costs sunk costs cash flow mutually exclusive projects"}, {"title": "08.02 · Free cash flow and discounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive cash flows to the firm and equity and choose consistent discount rates. FCFF FCFE reinvestment terminal value nominal-real consistency currency consistency"}, {"title": "08.03 · Valuation models and terminal assumptions", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value an operating business using explicit growth and reinvestment economics. DCF perpetuities value drivers ROIC growth fade terminal constraints"}, {"title": "08.04 · Risk, cost of capital and WACC", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate financing costs and explain when WACC is inappropriate. Cost of equity beta introduction debt costs market weights leverage project risk"}, {"title": "08.05 · Capital structure and financing frictions", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare financing policies under taxes, distress and incentive problems. Modigliani-Miller assumptions trade-off pecking order agency debt overhang"}, {"title": "08.06 · Payout, liquidity and financial policy", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Choose payout and liquidity policies consistent with investment and constraints. Dividends buybacks retained cash signaling working capital financial flexibility"}, {"title": "08.07 · Relative valuation and market evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use comparable valuations with economically meaningful denominators and peer choices. Enterprise-equity bridge multiples normalization comparability cycle effects triangulation"}, {"title": "08.08 · Real options and investment timing", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value flexibility and identify where static NPV understates contingent choices. Delay expand abandon staged investment decision trees information value"}, {"title": "08.09 · Mergers, acquisitions and control", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate acquisition economics and distinguish synergy from wealth transfers. Purchase price synergy integration control premiums financing dilution versus value"}, {"title": "08.10 · Private equity, venture capital and entrepreneurship", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze illiquid ownership structures and financing incentives. LBO venture rounds cap tables preferred claims exits fund economics selection"}, {"title": "08.11 · Distress, restructuring and governance", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare restructuring alternatives and their incentive and valuation consequences. Insolvency concepts priority recovery refinancing governance stakeholder conflicts"}, {"title": "08.12 · Corporate finance integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a financing and investment plan under alternative operating and market conditions. Integrated valuation capital allocation downside covenants scenario consistency board memo"}, {"title": "09.01 · Returns, payoffs and investment opportunity sets", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Measure investment outcomes with correct timing, distributions and benchmark conventions. Simple-log returns total return compounding cash flows gross-net returns opportunity sets"}, {"title": "09.02 · Portfolio mathematics and diversification", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compute portfolio risk and explain the role of covariance and concentration. Mean covariance matrix correlations diversification estimation error leverage"}, {"title": "09.03 · Mean-variance optimization and allocation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive efficient portfolios and assess sensitivity to estimated inputs. Efficient frontier minimum variance tangency constraints optimization shrinkage"}, {"title": "09.04 · CAPM and equilibrium risk pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive the CAPM mechanism and distinguish its theoretical assumptions from empirical evidence. Market portfolio beta security market line alpha equilibrium testing limitations"}, {"title": "09.05 · Arbitrage, state prices and discount factors", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Price contingent payoffs using no-arbitrage and stochastic discount-factor reasoning. Replication state prices pricing kernel completeness fundamental pricing equation"}, {"title": "09.06 · Factor models and empirical asset pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate factor exposures and distinguish explanatory fits from investable premiums. APT multifactor models cross-sectional pricing factor construction omitted factors"}, {"title": "09.07 · Market efficiency, anomalies and behavior", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate apparent predictability with information, risk and implementation costs in view. Efficiency forms joint hypothesis anomalies behavioral mechanisms limits to arbitrage"}, {"title": "09.08 · Active management and performance evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess whether performance reflects skill, risk, selection or luck. Benchmark choice alpha attribution information ratio persistence fees survivorship"}, {"title": "09.09 · Strategic and dynamic asset allocation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design allocation policies across horizons and investor constraints. Lifecycle liabilities hedging demand rebalancing tactical decisions estimation risk"}, {"title": "09.10 · Alternative assets and illiquidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare nontraditional investments with transparent valuation and liquidity assumptions. Real estate private markets commodities hedge funds appraisal smoothing lockups"}, {"title": "09.11 · Execution, trading costs and market microstructure", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate execution and liquidity effects on feasible investment strategies. Bid-ask spread impact order types information execution benchmarks capacity"}, {"title": "09.12 · Investment policy and portfolio integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a mandate and portfolio using economic objectives and robust evidence. IPS governance benchmarks risk budget costs stress scenarios reporting"}, {"title": "10.01 · Bond cash flows and yield curves", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Price cash flows with spot discount factors and distinguish yield conventions. Coupon schedules clean-dirty price discount curves YTM spot-forward rates day-count assumptions"}, {"title": "10.02 · Duration, convexity and immunization", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive rate sensitivity and construct hedges with explicit residual risks. Macaulay-modified duration convexity key rates immunization nonparallel shifts"}, {"title": "10.03 · Term-structure models and rate risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare curve models and distinguish physical from risk-neutral dynamics. Expectations term premiums short-rate models no-arbitrage calibration curve scenarios"}, {"title": "10.04 · Credit risk and recovery", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value defaultable claims and separate hazard, recovery and liquidity assumptions. Credit spreads structural models intensity recovery transition correlation"}, {"title": "10.05 · Forwards, futures and swaps", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive contract values and explain financing, collateral and basis effects. Cost of carry forward-futures distinction swaps collateral basis margin"}, {"title": "10.06 · Options and binomial replication", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Construct replicating portfolios and price options through state-contingent payoffs. Payoffs put-call parity binomial tree risk-neutral probability American exercise"}, {"title": "10.07 · Brownian motion and Ito calculus", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive stochastic differentials and explain quadratic variation. Filtrations Brownian motion stochastic integral Ito lemma SDE drift-diffusion"}, {"title": "10.08 · Black-Scholes and risk-neutral valuation", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive the pricing equation and distinguish a model's assumptions from market conventions. Replication PDE risk-neutral measure Black-Scholes formula dividends boundaries"}, {"title": "10.09 · Greeks, volatility and hedging", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze hedge sensitivities and the implications of changing volatility assumptions. Delta gamma vega theta implied volatility smiles discrete hedging"}, {"title": "10.10 · Numerical pricing and Monte Carlo", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement pricing methods with convergence and error diagnostics. Trees finite differences simulation variance reduction early exercise numerical checks"}, {"title": "10.11 · Incomplete markets and advanced derivatives", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain why some contingent claims cannot be uniquely priced by replication. Incompleteness stochastic volatility jumps exotic payoffs credit derivatives bounds"}, {"title": "10.12 · Quantitative model governance", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Validate pricing and risk models against independent benchmarks and failure scenarios. Calibration model risk implementation errors limitations validation controlled use"}, {"title": "11.01 · Financial intermediation and bank economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain the economic functions and fragilities of financial intermediaries. Screening monitoring maturity transformation deposits bank income capital"}, {"title": "11.02 · Bank balance sheets, capital and liquidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reconcile bank accounts and distinguish liquidity shortages from insolvency. Assets funding equity capital ratios concepts liquidity buffers accounting-economic value"}, {"title": "11.03 · Credit analysis and lending decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate creditworthiness using cash flow, incentives and downside protection. Debt service leverage covenants collateral repayment sources credit underwriting"}, {"title": "11.04 · Market risk and portfolio loss", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare risk measures and state their information and distributional limitations. VaR expected shortfall scenarios tails dependence horizon backtesting"}, {"title": "11.05 · Liquidity, funding and asset-liability management", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze funding gaps and contingent cash needs across scenarios. Liquidity ladders ALM rollover collateral margin maturity mismatch"}, {"title": "11.06 · Operational, legal and model risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design controls around human, operational and modeling failure mechanisms. Process controls cyber concepts fraud legal uncertainty model validation incident learning"}, {"title": "11.07 · Stress testing and risk aggregation", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build internally consistent stress scenarios without double counting exposures. Scenario design nonlinear losses contagion concentration reverse stress aggregation"}, {"title": "11.08 · Insurance, pensions and household finance", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate long-horizon liabilities and intermediary incentives. Insurance pooling adverse selection pensions longevity guarantees household credit"}, {"title": "11.09 · Currency, sovereign and cross-border risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze international claims with explicit currency and jurisdiction assumptions. FX exposure hedging sovereign debt capital flows country risk convertibility"}, {"title": "11.10 · Financial regulation and incentives", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate regulatory purposes and trade-offs using dated jurisdiction-specific sources. Prudential regulation resolution conduct systemic externalities arbitrage regulatory capture"}, {"title": "11.11 · Financial crises and systemic mechanisms", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace balance-sheet feedback, runs and contagion in a crisis model. Runs fire sales leverage cycles networks lender of last resort resolution"}, {"title": "11.12 · Risk governance and institutional integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an institution's risk appetite and response to a combined stress scenario. Risk limits board oversight escalation incentives decision logs contingency plans"}, {"title": "12.01 · Identification, estimands and asymptotic tools", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Separate what data identify from what an estimator computes and justify limiting arguments. Estimands identification consistency asymptotic normality delta method stochastic orders"}, {"title": "12.02 · Linear models and robust inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive linear estimators and choose inference appropriate to dependence and heterogeneity. OLS GLS heteroskedasticity clustering serial correlation finite-sample concerns"}, {"title": "12.03 · Instrumental variables and GMM", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive moment-based estimators and evaluate instrument relevance and validity. IV 2SLS weak instruments exclusion GMM overidentification local effects"}, {"title": "12.04 · Likelihood and nonlinear models", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate nonlinear models and distinguish functional-form identification from causal identification. MLE score information binary response count selection marginal effects"}, {"title": "12.05 · Panel data and longitudinal identification", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate within-unit identification and dynamic-panel assumptions. Fixed-random effects correlated heterogeneity dynamic panels attrition dependence"}, {"title": "12.06 · Experiments and causal inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design and evaluate interventions with appropriate estimands and assignment mechanisms. Potential outcomes randomization compliance interference attrition power randomization inference"}, {"title": "12.07 · Difference-in-differences and event studies", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend counterfactual trends and handle treatment timing and heterogeneous effects. Parallel trends anticipation staggered adoption event studies weighting sensitivity"}, {"title": "12.08 · Regression discontinuity and synthetic controls", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate local and comparative designs with transparent support and validity conditions. Continuity manipulation bandwidth fuzzy RD donor pools synthetic control placebo analysis"}, {"title": "12.09 · Time series, forecasting and financial econometrics", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze persistent and dependent economic series with model-specific inference. ARMA VAR unit roots cointegration volatility GARCH forecast evaluation"}, {"title": "12.10 · Structural estimation and economic mechanisms", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Connect economic models to data and distinguish calibration from identified estimation. Structural parameters moments simulation equilibrium indirect inference sensitivity"}, {"title": "12.11 · Bayesian, high-dimensional and machine-learning methods", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Apply modern methods while preserving valid inference and honest model evaluation. Bayesian computation regularization cross-fitting double robustness causal ML multiple testing"}, {"title": "12.12 · Econometric research audit", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Independently audit a complete empirical paper and defend methodological choices. Identification robustness inference specification search reproducibility external validity"}, {"title": "13.01 · Preference representation and choice theory", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Establish representation and choice results under precise axioms. Preferences continuity utility representation revealed preference rationalizability"}, {"title": "13.02 · Consumer and producer duality", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive duality identities and characterize economically admissible demand and cost functions. Expenditure profit integrability Slutsky symmetry homogeneity envelope identities"}, {"title": "13.03 · Uncertainty and information", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze risk attitudes and the economic value of information formally. Expected utility stochastic dominance risk aversion Bayesian information Blackwell ordering"}, {"title": "13.04 · General equilibrium and existence", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain and apply equilibrium existence arguments and their assumptions. Exchange-production economies fixed points excess demand Walras law existence uniqueness limits"}, {"title": "13.05 · Welfare, aggregation and social choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Prove welfare results and assess aggregation and distributional restrictions. Welfare theorems aggregation social welfare impossibility collective choice"}, {"title": "13.06 · Static games and refinements", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Establish strategic equilibria and analyze equilibrium selection. Mixed-strategy existence Bayesian games best responses refinements correlated equilibrium"}, {"title": "13.07 · Dynamic and repeated games", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze sequential incentives and intertemporal cooperation with credible strategies. Subgame perfection sequential equilibrium repeated games reputation folk-theorem conditions"}, {"title": "13.08 · Information economics and screening", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve screening and signaling models with participation and incentive constraints. Single crossing menus adverse selection signaling equilibrium selection"}, {"title": "13.09 · Contracts and dynamic incentives", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive optimal contracts under hidden action and limited commitment. Principal-agent sufficient statistics limited liability dynamic contracts relational incentives"}, {"title": "13.10 · Mechanism design and auctions", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive incentive-compatible allocation and payment rules with explicit informational assumptions. Revelation principle implementation optimal auctions incentive compatibility transfers"}, {"title": "13.11 · Matching, networks and market design", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze stable allocations and strategic interactions on markets and networks. Matching stability assignment network games externalities design constraints"}, {"title": "13.12 · Theory synthesis and research critique", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate a theoretical contribution through proofs, counterexamples and economic relevance. Model choice theorem scope proof checking robustness interpretation theoretical referee report"}, {"title": "14.01 · Dynamic optimization and recursive methods", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive Bellman and Euler conditions and establish valid numerical solutions. Bellman principle contraction Euler equations transversality policy iteration value iteration"}, {"title": "14.02 · Stochastic growth and real business cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve a stochastic growth model and evaluate its empirical mechanisms. Planner competitive equilibrium shocks labor perturbation calibration moments"}, {"title": "14.03 · Incomplete markets and heterogeneous agents", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze distributional state variables and precautionary decisions in incomplete markets. Aiyagari Huggett borrowing constraints distributions aggregation numerical methods"}, {"title": "14.04 · Search, matching and labor dynamics", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive dynamic labor-market equilibria and evaluate their implications. Matching functions bargaining vacancy creation unemployment dynamics shocks"}, {"title": "14.05 · New Keynesian models and monetary policy", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive a nominal-rigidity model and compare policy rules under explicit approximations. Household-firm problems Calvo NK Phillips curve dynamic IS Taylor rules determinacy"}, {"title": "14.06 · Fiscal policy and sovereign debt", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze dynamic public finance and sovereign default mechanisms. Government constraints fiscal-monetary interaction default sustainability redistribution"}, {"title": "14.07 · Consumption-based asset pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive stochastic discount factors and evaluate major pricing puzzles. Euler equation consumption risk equity premium risk-free rate habits long-run risks"}, {"title": "14.08 · Investment, debt and financial frictions", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive how financing constraints alter investment and aggregate dynamics. q theory agency collateral debt overhang financial accelerator balance sheets"}, {"title": "14.09 · Intermediaries, liquidity and leverage cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze financial-sector amplification and endogenous risk. Intermediary capital leverage liquidity runs risk-bearing capacity feedback"}, {"title": "14.10 · International macro-finance", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve open-economy dynamic models with financial and exchange-rate mechanisms. Risk sharing sudden stops sovereign risk currencies global financial conditions"}, {"title": "14.11 · Quantitative solution and model evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess macroeconomic models through transparent estimation and numerical diagnostics. Perturbation global methods likelihood-moment methods identification validation sensitivity"}, {"title": "14.12 · Macro-finance research frontier", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Critically compare frontier contributions and develop a tractable research question. Heterogeneity financial networks monetary transmission intermediary pricing current literature protocol"}, {"title": "15.01 · Public economics and taxation", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze tax and spending decisions with efficiency and distribution in view. Tax incidence optimal taxation public goods social insurance welfare weights"}, {"title": "15.02 · Labor economics and human capital", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate wage, employment and education mechanisms using credible designs. Human capital returns to education labor supply-demand discrimination mobility"}, {"title": "15.03 · Industrial organization and competition", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess market conduct and competitive policy using models and evidence. Demand estimation entry pricing merger analysis platforms market definition"}, {"title": "15.04 · Development, poverty and structural change", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare poverty-reduction mechanisms and their institutional constraints. Poverty measurement credit health education structural transformation field experiments"}, {"title": "15.05 · International trade and global production", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze firm and worker responses to trade and production networks. Heterogeneous firms trade costs global value chains tariffs distribution adjustment"}, {"title": "15.06 · Political economy and institutions", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze collective decisions and institutional incentives with defensible identification. Voting accountability lobbying bureaucracy state capacity capture"}, {"title": "15.07 · Economic history and historical inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use historical evidence to distinguish mechanisms and avoid anachronistic causal claims. Industrialization institutions crises historical data natural experiments comparative inference"}, {"title": "15.08 · Behavioral and experimental economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design and critique evidence about nonstandard preferences and beliefs. Behavioral models experiments replication external validity welfare intervention"}, {"title": "15.09 · Environmental, energy and climate economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate externalities and long-horizon choices under uncertainty. Carbon pricing discounting resource economics damages energy markets distribution"}, {"title": "15.10 · Health, education and demographic economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess service provision and demographic change through incentives and evidence. Insurance health markets education production fertility aging public policy"}, {"title": "15.11 · Urban economics, housing and inequality", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze spatial decisions and distribution with equilibrium feedback. Location housing supply amenities sorting migration inequality mobility"}, {"title": "15.12 · Policy synthesis and institutional feasibility", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an implementable policy with evidence, uncertainty and distributional consequences. Cost-benefit political constraints administrative capacity counterfactuals monitoring evaluation"}, {"title": "16.01 · Choosing a field and a research agenda", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Select a tractable specialization and define an economically significant question. Comparative advantage scope feasibility contribution field boundaries macro-finance default"}, {"title": "16.02 · Literature mapping and critical reading", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Map a field by questions, methods and disagreements rather than accumulating citations. Search strategy seminal papers frontier updates evidence tables citation chains"}, {"title": "16.03 · Replication and reproduction", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reproduce a research result and document discrepancies without overstating their meaning. Data provenance code reconstruction measurement versions replication limits reporting"}, {"title": "16.04 · Referee reports and research judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write a fair report that distinguishes fatal identification issues from improvements. Contribution correctness evidence novelty major-minor concerns constructive criticism"}, {"title": "16.05 · Question design and identification strategy", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Formulate a research question with an explicit path from data or assumptions to conclusions. Estimand mechanism identification counterfactual feasibility alternative explanations"}, {"title": "16.06 · Theory building and model discipline", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Develop a parsimonious model that produces testable or interpretable implications. Baseline assumptions equilibrium comparative statics falsifiable implications extensions"}, {"title": "16.07 · Data construction, measurement and ethics", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build a defensible research dataset with documented measurement and access constraints. Collection linkage proxies privacy consent concepts missingness research integrity"}, {"title": "16.08 · Research design, power and preregistration", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Plan an empirical study that separates design from outcome-driven specification choices. Power assignment analysis plan preregistration multiple outcomes contingency decisions"}, {"title": "16.09 · Robustness, uncertainty and external validity", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate whether a conclusion survives plausible alternative assumptions and populations. Sensitivity robustness specification curves transportability structural stability limits"}, {"title": "16.10 · Writing a scholarly paper", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Organize a paper around its question, contribution, evidence and limitations. Abstract introduction methods results tables appendices transparent claims"}, {"title": "16.11 · Seminars, revision and oral defense", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend research under probing questions and revise based on justified criticism. Presentation questions response memo revision defense disagreement"}, {"title": "16.12 · Independent research portfolio", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assemble original work and an evaluation protocol for independent expert judgment. Original contribution reproducibility ethics audit external review research agenda next papers"}, {"title": "17.01 · Learning objectives and prerequisite design", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate disciplinary knowledge into assessable learning capabilities. Objectives prerequisite maps cognitive load course alignment transfer"}, {"title": "17.02 · Explaining intuition, mathematics and evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Teach a concept across levels without changing its meaning or hiding assumptions. First principles representations notation derivation empirical interpretation analogies"}, {"title": "17.03 · Designing an undergraduate course", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build a coherent course from prerequisites to cumulative assessments. Syllabus sequencing pacing assignments readings integration"}, {"title": "17.04 · Designing a graduate seminar", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Organize a research seminar around questions, methods and scholarly disagreement. Paper selection presentations discussion critique replication research proposals"}, {"title": "17.05 · Problem design and meaningful assessment", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write assessments that diagnose reasoning and transfer rather than template recognition. Difficulty validity original problems unseen transfer rubrics exam security"}, {"title": "17.06 · Grading, feedback and remediation", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Grade consistently and give feedback that identifies the next learning action. Calibration fairness partial credit misconceptions remediation reassessment"}, {"title": "17.07 · Teaching cases and leading discussion", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Lead analytical discussion while making assumptions and evidence visible. Cases questioning participation alternative decisions synthesis facilitation"}, {"title": "17.08 · Misconceptions and learning evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Diagnose persistent misunderstandings and evaluate whether instruction changed them. Diagnostic questions retrieval formative feedback retention learning measurement"}, {"title": "17.09 · Research supervision and mentoring", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Guide research toward independence with explicit responsibilities and ethical boundaries. Scope milestones feedback authorship conflict mentorship independence"}, {"title": "17.10 · Communication to policymakers and boards", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate technical findings into decisions without overstating certainty. Executive memos uncertainty graphs trade-offs audience actionable recommendations"}, {"title": "17.11 · Observed teaching and reflective practice", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate teaching using observation and evidence of learner understanding. Observation rubric peer feedback student work revision accessibility reflection"}, {"title": "17.12 · Teaching and supervision portfolio", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a complete teaching program and its assessment evidence. Syllabi materials teaching demonstration exam design supervision plan external evaluation"}, {"title": "18.01 · Institutional purpose and strategy", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate an institution's mission into coherent choices and resource priorities. Mission stakeholders advantage trade-offs strategy success measures"}, {"title": "18.02 · Resource allocation and institutional budgets", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build budgets and allocate resources under opportunity cost and uncertainty. Operating budgets capital budgets endowments research funding P&L scenario planning"}, {"title": "18.03 · Organizations, incentives and culture", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design organizational responsibilities and incentives that support desired behavior. Structure principal-agent delegation culture coordination measurement unintended incentives"}, {"title": "18.04 · Hiring, evaluation and talent development", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build defensible hiring and development processes for expert organizations. Selection evidence evaluation promotion feedback succession bias controls"}, {"title": "18.05 · Negotiation, conflict and stakeholder decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Negotiate institutional decisions using incentives, alternatives and principled process. BATNA interests bargaining coalition conflict resolution contracts"}, {"title": "18.06 · Governance, fiduciary duties and integrity", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Map decision rights and accountability using jurisdiction-aware legal and ethical analysis. Boards duties conflicts disclosure academic freedom research integrity escalation"}, {"title": "18.07 · Academic institution leadership", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design academic strategy while protecting scholarship and educational standards. Faculty curriculum research funding admissions governance donors endowment autonomy"}, {"title": "18.08 · Financial institution leadership", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Integrate business strategy, capital, liquidity, risk and operating accountability. Business model capital allocation risk appetite controls compensation regulatory relations"}, {"title": "18.09 · Operations, innovation and change", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement organizational improvements with measurable feedback and adaptation. Process bottlenecks change experimentation technology AI evaluation execution"}, {"title": "18.10 · Crisis leadership and high-stakes judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Make and revise accountable crisis decisions with incomplete information. Triage uncertainty scenarios communication decision logs contingency after-action review"}, {"title": "18.11 · Real responsibility and leadership evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess leadership through observed decisions and outcomes rather than self-description. Project ownership people budgets delivery external feedback retrospective attribution"}, {"title": "18.12 · Institutional leadership portfolio and final integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an integrated strategy and demonstrate the limits and strengths of one's preparation. Academic-financial cases governance research teaching capital risk external evaluation continuing development"}];
